/* Domain 54 — Freelancing, IT Consulting & Running a Technical Business (facts as of October 2026; general education, not legal or tax advice) */
BOOTCAMP.phases.push({
id:"p54", num:54, title:"Freelancing, IT Consulting & Running a Technical Business", short:"Tech Business",
tagline:"Turn technical skill into a business that pays you predictably: choose the model, scope and price the work, sign the right paper, run managed services that do not break, sell security and AI responsibly, and grow without burning out.",
prereq:"Domain 26 (IT operations, service management, ITIL, tickets and SLAs) is required. Domain 45 (technical project management: scope, estimates, change control, contract types) and Domain 33 (governance, risk, compliance and privacy) are recommended; Modules 2–4 and 6 lean on them. A spreadsheet, a docs editor and a free time tracker and invoicing app are the only tools you need. Nothing here is legal or tax advice: Module 4 says exactly when to hire a lawyer or CPA.",
outcomes:[
"Choose and combine business models (freelance, consulting, agency, productized service, MSP and MSSP) and compute MRR, churn, gross margin, CAC, LTV and payback for each service line.",
"Find and qualify clients in a chosen niche, run a discovery call, engineer requirements with acceptance criteria, and write proposals and statements of work with change control built in.",
"Set prices from first principles: the rate formula from target income, utilization and overhead; fixed and value pricing; estimates as ranges; and operating-cost models for hosting and AI services, including API costs versus amortized local hardware.",
"Explain US and Texas business basics (sole proprietorship versus LLC, the Certificate of Formation, franchise tax, sales tax on data processing, estimated taxes, contracts, IP and insurance) and know exactly when to hire a lawyer or CPA.",
"Deliver managed IT and hosting reliably: SLAs, SLOs and error budgets, monitoring and alerting, ticketing, RMM and PSA tools, tested backups, patching, incident communication, runbooks and clean client offboarding.",
"Run security engagements only under written authorization and agreed rules of engagement, serve HIPAA and PCI DSS clients correctly, and design AI receptionists and automations that respect recording-consent law, retention limits and vendor agreements.",
"Grow deliberately: positioning, reputation and case studies, referral partners, subcontracting and the first hire, automation, the choice to stay small, and a one-page operating plan reviewed every quarter.",
"Prepare for ITIL AI Governance (Version 5) and plan the path to the IMC USA Certified Management Consultant (CMC) credential."
],
texts:["Managing the Professional Service Firm — David H. Maister (Free Press, 1993): leverage, utilization and client relationships","Flawless Consulting: A Guide to Getting Your Expertise Used — Peter Block (3rd edition, Pfeiffer, 2011)","Site Reliability Engineering — Beyer, Jones, Petoff & Murphy (O'Reilly, 2016; free at sre.google), chapter 4, Service Level Objectives","U.S. Small Business Administration business guide (sba.gov), with the Texas Secretary of State and Texas Comptroller guides for business owners"],
modules:[
{id:"p54m1", title:"Business Models in Technical Services: Freelance, Consulting, MSP, MSSP and Productized Offers", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Distinguish freelance, consulting, agency, productized, MSP and MSSP models by what the client buys, how revenue arrives and who carries the risk.","Explain why managed services replaced break-fix, using the incentive each model creates.","Compute MRR, churn, gross margin, CAC, LTV and payback for a managed-service client.","Apply Maister's profit equation (margin × productivity × leverage) to a one-person firm and to its first hire.","Map each of your service lines to a model and design a revenue mix in which projects feed recurring plans."],
body:`<p>What exactly are you selling, and how does the money arrive? Two shops with the same skills, the same city and the same clients can end up five years apart: one exhausted and still trading hours, the other with a predictable monthly base and time to think. The difference is the business model, and you choose it, deliberately or by accident, every time you write an offer. Golden Rock already sells five things: websites and hosting, AI receptionists and automation, IT services, cybersecurity services and AI implementation. Each fits a different model. This module gives you the vocabulary and the arithmetic to run them as one business instead of five side jobs. Every number below belongs to a <em>model agency</em> invented for teaching; in Lab 1 you replace each one with your own.</p>
<h4>Six models, by what the client actually buys</h4>
<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Client buys</th><th>Revenue shape</th><th>Risk sits with</th><th>Example from your lines</th></tr>
<tr><td>Freelance</td><td>Your hours</td><td>Hourly or daily invoices</td><td>Client (pays for time)</td><td>"Fix our WordPress site"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consulting</td><td>A diagnosis, decision or plan</td><td>Fixed-fee engagements, retainers</td><td>Shared</td><td>"AI readiness assessment and roadmap"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agency project</td><td>A finished deliverable</td><td>Milestone payments</td><td>You, if fixed price</td><td>"New website in six weeks"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Productized service</td><td>A fixed package: scope, price, timeline</td><td>Setup fee plus monthly</td><td>You, but the scope is fixed</td><td>"AI receptionist live in ten business days"</td></tr>
<tr><td>MSP</td><td>Systems that keep working</td><td>Monthly, per user or per device</td><td>You (flat fee)</td><td>"Managed IT for a 15-person office"</td></tr>
<tr><td>MSSP</td><td>Security monitoring and response</td><td>Monthly, per endpoint or user</td><td>You and your SOC partner</td><td>"Managed detection and response"</td></tr></table>
<p>A freelancer sells time and the client manages the work. A consultant sells judgment: the deliverable is a decision the client could not make alone, and Peter Block's <em>Flawless Consulting</em> is blunt that the consultant has influence but no direct control, so the work is only done when the client acts. An agency sells a deliverable and manages the work itself. An <strong>MSP</strong> (managed service provider) takes ongoing responsibility for a client's IT for a flat monthly fee, and an <strong>MSSP</strong> (managed security service provider) does the same for security monitoring, detection and response, which in practice means a 24×7 capability that a one-person firm can only offer through a partner (Module 7). These are not rungs on a ladder. Most successful small firms run two or three at once, and the craft is in making them feed each other.</p>
<h4>Break-fix versus managed: follow the incentive</h4>
<p>For decades small-business IT was <em>break-fix</em>: something breaks, the client calls, you bill hours. Look at the incentive. The provider earns more when the client's systems fail, and the client is tempted to delay calling until the problem is expensive. The managed model inverts it: you earn the same fee whether the client opens three tickets this month or thirty, so every outage prevented by patching, monitoring and tested backups (Module 5) is profit. That alignment, plus the remote monitoring and management tools that made proactive work cheap, is why the MSP model displaced break-fix across the industry. The price of the inversion is that you now own the risk of a client who generates too much work. The defenses are a precise scope (which users, devices and hours are covered; what counts as a project billed separately), per-user pricing that grows with the client, and onboarding standards that refuse to support an unpatched mess at a flat fee.</p>
<h4>Recurring revenue and the arithmetic of churn</h4>
<p><strong>Recurring revenue</strong> repeats under a standing agreement without a new sale: hosting and care plans, managed IT, AI receptionist subscriptions, a monthly <strong>retainer</strong>. Measure it as <strong>MRR</strong>, monthly recurring revenue. The model agency has 12 care plans at $79, 3 AI receptionist clients at $449 and 2 managed IT clients at $1,500: MRR = $948 + $1,347 + $3,000 = $5,295, or $63,540 a year. Project revenue in the same month might be $9,000 from one website and $0 the next. Recurring revenue is worth more than the same dollars of project revenue because it is predictable: you can hire against it, it smooths cash flow, and buyers of service businesses value it far above one-off fees.</p>
<p><strong>Churn</strong> is the fraction of recurring revenue (or clients) lost per month, and it compounds. Retention after a year is (1 − c)<sup>12</sup>. At 2% a month, 0.98<sup>12</sup> ≈ 0.785, so you lose 21.5% of the base every year and must sell that much just to stand still; at 1% you lose 11.4%. Net revenue retention adds back upgrades: a care-plan client who adds an AI receptionist offsets two lost hosting plans. The cheapest growth you will ever buy is lower churn, and churn is mostly a delivery problem: responsiveness, reliability and a monthly report that proves you did something.</p>
<h4>Unit economics: does one more client make you richer?</h4>
<p><strong>Unit economics</strong> asks whether a single client is profitable on its own once every cost of serving it is counted. Five numbers do the work:</p>
<ul><li><em>ARPA</em>, average revenue per account per month.</li>
<li><strong>Gross margin</strong> = (revenue − direct costs) ÷ revenue, where direct costs are the per-seat tools and the labor, at <em>loaded</em> cost (Module 3), that grow with that client.</li>
<li><strong>Customer acquisition cost</strong> (CAC) = sales and marketing spend in a period, including your own selling time, ÷ new clients won.</li>
<li>Payback = CAC ÷ monthly gross profit, in months.</li>
<li><strong>Lifetime value</strong> (LTV) = monthly gross profit ÷ monthly churn.</li></ul>
<p>Worked: a managed IT client with 12 users at $100 per user is $1,200 a month. Tools (RMM, endpoint protection, backup, documentation) run about $22 per user, $264. Labor averages 0.8 hours per user per month, 9.6 hours at a loaded $45, $432. Gross profit is $504, a gross margin of 42%. Winning the client took $1,800 of ads plus 20 hours of your selling time valued at $50, so CAC = $2,800 and payback = 2,800 ÷ 504 ≈ 5.6 months. With 1.5% monthly churn, LTV = 504 ÷ 0.015 = $33,600, about 12 times CAC. Read the result critically. The LTV formula assumes constant churn, and with five clients one loss is 20% churn, so treat LTV as a range. A 42% margin leaves little room for the overhead and profit that Module 3 adds; the fix is price, scope or labor efficiency, never more volume of the same.</p>
<h4>Leverage: Maister's profit equation</h4>
<p>David Maister's <em>Managing the Professional Service Firm</em> reduced professional-services economics to one identity: profit per partner = (profit ÷ fees) × (fees ÷ professionals) × (professionals ÷ partners), that is, margin × productivity × leverage. Solo, leverage is 1, so profit can rise only through margin (price and cost) or productivity (utilization × rate), and both have ceilings. Leverage comes from three places: people (subcontractors and hires, Module 7), process (the same AI receptionist build, templated and checklisted), and software (RMM scripts, automations, a local model on your own hardware drafting the first version, as in Domain 12). Maister's roles are finders who win work, minders who manage clients and grinders who do it. Alone you are all three, which is why a solo owner's billable share of the week rarely exceeds 60–65% (Module 3).</p>
<h4>Productized services: expertise sold like a product</h4>
<p>A <strong>productized service</strong> has a fixed scope, fixed price, fixed timeline, a named deliverable and a repeatable process, and it can be bought from a web page. "AI receptionist launch: $1,500 setup, live in ten business days; greeting and call-flow script, a knowledge base of up to 40 answers, one calendar integration, call summaries by email; then $449 a month including 500 minutes." Productizing turns estimates into measurements (you have done it twelve times), shortens the sales cycle because the buyer compares one price instead of a proposal, and makes the work delegable. The risk is one-size-fits-none: requests outside the box are change orders (Module 2), not favors.</p>
<h4>Your service lines, mapped</h4>
<table><tr><th>Service line</th><th>Natural model</th><th>Recurring hook</th><th>Watch for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Websites</td><td>Agency project or productized</td><td>Hosting plus care plan</td><td>Open-ended revisions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Recurring, MSP-like</td><td>Monthly per site</td><td>Being on call for a server you do not control</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI receptionists, automation</td><td>Productized plus subscription</td><td>Monthly with usage tiers</td><td>Per-minute costs that scale with use (Module 3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>IT services</td><td>MSP</td><td>Per user per month</td><td>Unlimited support with no scope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cybersecurity</td><td>Consulting (assessments) plus MSSP through a partner</td><td>Monitoring subscription</td><td>Authorization and 24×7 promises (Module 6)</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI implementation</td><td>Consulting plus project</td><td>Retainer for tuning and support</td><td>Promising outcomes a model cannot guarantee</td></tr></table>
<p>The strategy that follows is <em>land with a project, expand to a plan</em>: the website becomes hosting plus care, the IT cleanup becomes managed IT, the AI pilot becomes a support retainer. Track one ratio monthly: coverage = MRR ÷ fixed monthly costs (overhead plus your minimum draw). The model agency has $5,295 against $2,400 of overhead and a $4,000 minimum draw, a coverage of 0.83. Above 1.0 you survive a month with no new project; below it every slow month is a crisis. Raising coverage past 1.0 is the first strategic goal of a small technical business.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>ITIL AI Governance (Version 5) opens with how AI creates value, for whom, and who is accountable for it (its foundations and benefits-and-risks areas); the ITIL Foundation you met in Domain 26 frames the same offers as services that co-create value with consumers through service relationships. The CMC's core-competency exam and panel interview test the business of consulting itself: engagement economics, independence and the client relationship.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Where should your next 20 hours a month go?</strong> The model agency compares contribution per owner-hour, (revenue − non-labor direct costs) ÷ hours, across four offers. Website project: $6,000 fee, $150 of plugins and stock assets, 45 hours, so (6,000 − 150) ÷ 45 = $130 per hour, once. Care plan: $79 a month, $6 of server and backup share, 25 minutes, so 73 ÷ 0.417 ≈ $175 per hour, every month. AI receptionist subscription: $449, $110 of telephony, speech and model usage, 1.5 hours of tuning and reporting, so 339 ÷ 1.5 = $226 per hour, every month. Managed IT, 12 users: $1,200, $264 of tools, 9.6 hours, so 936 ÷ 9.6 = $97.50 per hour. Managed IT looks worst, but it has the largest ARPA, the lowest churn and work a $28-an-hour technician can do later, so it is the line that scales with leverage. The decision: put the next 20 hours into AI receptionists and care plans (high contribution, needs your expertise), keep websites as the landing offer, and reprice managed IT to $125 per user at the next renewal while scripting the routine checks to cut labor to 0.6 hours per user. Coverage would move from 0.83 to (5,295 + 4 × 449 + 10 × 79 + 2 × 300) ÷ 6,400 ≈ 1.33.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Recurring revenue is passive income.\" It is a promise to deliver every month; it is only valuable if the margin survives the support load and churn stays low.","\"Hourly billing is the safe model.\" It caps income at hours × rate, punishes your own efficiency and turns every invoice into an argument about time instead of results.","\"More clients always means more profit.\" Only when each client's unit economics are positive after loaded labor and tools; a negative-margin client costs more the better you serve it."],
problems:[
{q:"You have $8,000 MRR and lose 2.5% of it every month. How much new MRR must you add each month to reach $10,000 in twelve months?", a:"After 12 months the existing base is 8,000 × 0.975¹² ≈ 8,000 × 0.738 = $5,904. New monthly additions a accumulate as a × (1 − 0.738) ÷ 0.025 ≈ 10.48a. Solve 5,904 + 10.48a = 10,000: a ≈ $391 of new MRR every month, before any growth from upgrades."},
{q:"A client has 25 users at $110 per user. Tools cost $20 per user, labor averages 0.7 hours per user at $48 loaded, CAC was $4,500 and monthly churn is 2%. Compute gross margin, payback, LTV and LTV:CAC, and say what you would check before trusting them.", a:"Revenue $2,750; tools $500; labor 17.5 h × $48 = $840; gross profit $1,410; gross margin 51.3%. Payback 4,500 ÷ 1,410 ≈ 3.2 months. LTV = 1,410 ÷ 0.02 = $70,500; LTV:CAC ≈ 15.7. Check whether 0.7 hours per user is measured or guessed (time-tracker data), whether churn comes from enough clients to mean anything, and whether projects are leaking into the flat fee."},
{q:"A 10-person law office has paid you break-fix at $125 an hour, averaging 6 hours a month with two bad months of 20 hours. Design a managed offer and justify its price.", a:"Average spend is about $750 a month but volatile. Offer managed IT at about $95 per user ($950 a month) covering monitoring, patching, endpoint protection, backups with quarterly restore tests and remote support for covered users in business hours; projects and new hardware billed separately. Tools run about $200. The client pays above its average because it buys predictability and prevention; you profit by preventing the 20-hour months."},
{q:"A solo consultant bills $160,000 with $64,000 profit. She adds a technician who bills $70,000 and costs $50,000 loaded. Compute margin, productivity, leverage and profit per partner before and after.", a:"Before: margin 0.40, productivity $160,000, leverage 1, profit $64,000. After: fees $230,000, profit $84,000, margin ≈ 0.365, productivity $115,000 per professional, leverage 2; 0.365 × 115,000 × 2 ≈ $84,000. Margin and productivity fell but leverage doubled, and profit rose $20,000."},
{q:"Productize 'we do AI automation.' Give the name, scope, price, timeline, exclusions, and the metric that proves it works.", a:"Example: 'Missed-lead rescue: an automation that texts every missed call within 60 seconds and books a callback.' Scope: one phone line, one CRM or calendar, two message templates, a weekly report. $900 setup, live in 7 business days, $149 a month. Exclusions: custom integrations, more lines, marketing content. Proof: missed calls answered within 60 seconds and callbacks booked per month, against a baseline measured before launch."}
],
checks:[{q:"What does an MSP sell, and how is it usually priced?",a:"Ongoing responsibility for systems that keep working, for a flat monthly fee per user or per device."},{q:"Formula for LTV?",a:"Monthly gross profit per client ÷ monthly churn rate."},{q:"Maister's three profit levers?",a:"Margin, productivity (utilization × rate) and leverage."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why break-fix and managed services create opposite incentives, then walk her through the unit economics of one managed client (revenue, direct costs, gross margin, CAC, payback and LTV) using numbers from your own business."},

{id:"p54m2", title:"Finding Clients and Scoping Work: Niches, Discovery Calls, Requirements, Proposals, SOWs and Change Control", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Choose a niche by industry and problem and write an ideal-client profile that filters leads.","Run a discovery call that quantifies the client's pain, decision process, budget and success criteria.","Turn discovery notes into requirements with MoSCoW priorities and testable acceptance criteria.","Write a proposal with options and a statement of work with scope, exclusions, deliverables, client responsibilities and payment terms.","Run change control with change orders priced and signed before the work starts."],
body:`<p>Who should you work for, what exactly will you do for them, and how will both of you know it is done? Most failed client projects are lost before any work starts: the wrong client, a vague scope, or a change nobody priced. Domain 45 taught requirements, the WBS and change control inside an organization. Here the other party is a customer with a checkbook, and every ambiguity becomes an argument about money. The documents in this module are not bureaucracy; they are how a small firm stays paid and stays friends with its clients.</p>
<h4>A niche: an industry times a problem</h4>
<p>A niche is an industry (dental practices, law firms, plumbing and HVAC contractors, property managers) crossed with a problem you solve repeatably (missed calls, aging IT, a website that does not convert, compliance anxiety). Specializing feels like turning work away; in practice it pays four ways. Your assets become reusable: the third dental website reuses the scheduling integration, the HIPAA checklist and the case study from the first two. Referrals compound, because owners in one trade talk to each other. You can charge more, because a specialist is compared with other specialists, not with a teenager on a freelance marketplace. And marketing gets easier, because you know where those owners gather and what they fear. Golden Rock sells beyond Texas, which shapes the choice: remote services (websites, AI receptionists, automation) travel across state lines, while hands-on IT support does not. A sensible split is a productized remote offer for one industry nationally and managed IT for local San Antonio clients.</p>
<p>Write the <em>ideal client profile</em> down: industry, size (say 5 to 50 employees), geography, budget signals, trigger events (a receptionist quits, a breach scare, a new office, a website that stopped bringing calls), and disqualifiers (no decision-maker reachable, wants the cheapest option, a history of firing every vendor). Every lead gets checked against it before you spend an hour.</p>
<h4>The pipeline, in numbers</h4>
<p>Sales is a funnel, and a funnel is arithmetic. The model agency's last quarter: 40 leads, 16 discovery calls (40%), 8 proposals (50%), 3 signed (37.5%), a lead-to-client rate of 7.5%. To add two clients a month it needs about 2 ÷ 0.075 ≈ 27 leads a month, or better conversion. Each stage has its own fix: more leads is marketing (Module 7); more calls is qualification and fast response; more proposals is a better discovery call; more signatures is a better proposal. Qualify early with the old IBM checklist, BANT: budget, authority, need, timeline. A lead that fails two of the four goes to a polite nurture email, not a proposal. A common planning rule is to keep open proposals worth about three times the revenue you need to close.</p>
<h4>The discovery call</h4>
<p>A <strong>discovery call</strong> is a structured 30 to 45 minute conversation in which you learn whether there is a problem worth solving, what it costs the client, and how they buy. You talk at most a third of the time. A reliable agenda:</p>
<ul><li><em>Situation</em>: what they do, size, current tools, who handles IT or the phones today.</li>
<li><em>Problem</em>: what is not working, since when, a recent concrete example.</li>
<li><em>Impact</em>: put a number on it. How many calls go to voicemail in a week? What is an average job worth? How many hours does the office lose when the server is down?</li>
<li><em>Outcome</em>: "Six months from now, what is different?" These become success criteria.</li>
<li><em>Constraints</em>: budget range (ask plainly: "projects like this usually run $6,000 to $9,000; is that workable?"), deadlines, regulation (patient data, card payments, call recording), existing contracts.</li>
<li><em>Decision</em>: who decides, who signs, who else is affected, and how they chose their last vendor.</li>
<li><em>Next step</em>: agreed, with a date, before you hang up.</li></ul>
<p>Within 24 hours send a discovery summary in the client's own words: the problem, the numbers, the outcome they want and the next step. It proves you listened and becomes the first page of the proposal. Do not quote a price on the call for anything unclear. The cone of uncertainty from Domain 20 applies (Module 3 puts numbers on it): at first contact an estimate can be off by a factor of four either way. For complex work, sell a paid assessment first, so the client pays for the discovery that makes the estimate trustworthy.</p>
<h4>Requirements engineering for clients</h4>
<p>Turn notes into requirements the way Domain 45 taught, with one change: each requirement must be testable by someone who does not trust you yet. <em>Functional</em> requirements say what it does ("the receptionist books appointments into the shared calendar"). <em>Non-functional</em> requirements say how well ("answers in under two rings, 24×7; each reply starts within 1.5 seconds; transcripts deleted after 30 days; website pages pass WCAG 2.2 AA", from Domain 44). <em>Constraints</em> come from outside ("Florida callers must hear a recording disclosure", Module 6). Prioritize with MoSCoW (Must, Should, Could, Won't this time), which is how a $12,000 wish list fits an $8,000 budget without anyone feeling cheated.</p>
<p>Every deliverable then gets <strong>acceptance criteria</strong>: conditions with an observable pass or fail. Written as Given/When/Then: "Given a caller asks for a weekday appointment, when a slot is free, then the receptionist books it in the shared calendar, sends an SMS confirmation within one minute, and the call summary lists the booking." Managed IT has its own requirements: an inventory of users, devices, operating systems, licenses, network gear, backups, admin credentials and vendors (Domains 26 and 30). AI work needs a data inventory (Domain 33): what data, whose, where it flows, how long it is kept.</p>
<h4>Proposals that get signed</h4>
<p>A proposal is a decision document, not a brochure. Two to four pages: their situation in their words, the outcomes and how you will measure them, options, price and payment, timeline, why you, and the next step with an expiry date (30 days is usual). Offer three options. The middle one is what you recommend; the top one anchors the price and occasionally sells; the bottom one keeps a budget-limited client from walking away. The client stops asking "should we hire them?" and starts asking "which one?"</p>
<h4>The statement of work</h4>
<p>The <strong>statement of work</strong> (SOW) defines one project. It sits under a <strong>master services agreement</strong> (MSA) that holds the legal terms every project inherits: liability, IP, confidentiality, payment and disputes (Module 4). Sign the MSA once; write a short SOW per project.</p>
<table><tr><th>SOW section</th><th>What it says</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>Objectives</td><td>Business outcomes and success measures</td><td>Anchors every later decision</td></tr>
<tr><td>In scope</td><td>Deliverables, listed</td><td>Exactly what you owe</td></tr>
<tr><td>Out of scope</td><td>Explicit exclusions</td><td>Stops scope creep before it starts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Criteria per deliverable; acceptance period (say 5 business days, silence counts as acceptance)</td><td>Lets the project end</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule</td><td>Milestones that depend on client inputs</td><td>A client delay moves the dates, in writing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Client responsibilities</td><td>Content, access, decisions, one point of contact</td><td>Most delays start here</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assumptions</td><td>Two revision rounds; client supplies copy and photos</td><td>Turns hidden expectations into terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fees and payment</td><td>50% deposit, 40% at launch, 10% at acceptance; net 15</td><td>Cash flow; you never finance the client</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change process</td><td>How changes are requested, priced and approved</td><td>The next section</td></tr></table>
<h4>Change control and scope creep</h4>
<p><strong>Scope creep</strong> is scope that grows without a matching change in price, schedule or approval. Its antidote is the <strong>change order</strong>: a signed amendment to the SOW that describes the change, its price and its effect on the schedule. No work starts until it is signed. The process is Domain 45's change control at small scale: log the request, assess the impact, price it, get the signature, update the schedule, then do the work. For a stream of small requests, sell a prepaid bank (five hours at your rate, drawn down and reported) so you are not writing a form for every font change. The script is simple and friendly: "Happy to add that. It is outside the current scope, so I will send a change order today: $600 and two extra days. Shall I go ahead?" Managed services need the same boundary between <em>support</em> (covered by the monthly fee) and <em>projects</em> (a new office, a migration, a new line of business), written into the MSA's service description.</p>
<p>Watch for red flags while scoping: no decision-maker in any meeting; "it's a simple site, it shouldn't take long"; requests for free spec work; unlimited revisions; contempt for the previous vendor; slow payment of the assessment invoice. Each predicts a dispute. Saying no early is cheaper than saying sorry late.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>The CMC panel interview asks you to present one engagement and explain how you contracted it, which is Block's first phase of consulting (entry and contracting, followed by discovery and dialogue, feedback and the decision to act, engagement and implementation, and extension or termination). ITIL AI Governance's improvement model begins the same way: assess the current state and define requirements and accountabilities before designing anything. Project+ (Domain 45) tests the same SOW, scope and change-control vocabulary.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>From discovery to signed SOW for a fictional out-of-state client.</strong> Bayou Line Plumbing in Pensacola, Florida, has six technicians and about 220 inbound calls a week. On the discovery call the owner estimates 18% go to voicemail at lunch and after hours, about 40 calls, and that roughly 35% of those callers book a competitor; an average job is $380. Value at risk: 40 × 0.35 × $380 ≈ $5,320 a week, about $23,000 a month. Requirements: Must answer 24×7, book into their scheduling software, play a recording disclosure in the greeting (Florida requires the consent of all parties, Module 6) and hand emergencies to the on-call technician. Should send SMS confirmations. Could handle Spanish. Won't make outbound sales calls (federal robocall rules, Module 6). The proposal offers Essentials ($1,500 setup + $449 a month, 500 minutes), Recommended (adds SMS confirmations and a weekly missed-revenue report, $1,900 + $549) and Complete (adds a website refresh, $7,400 + $628). The owner signs Recommended. The SOW lists five deliverables with Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, client responsibilities (API access, a price sheet, an on-call roster within 3 business days), payment of 50% at signing and 50% at go-live, and two weeks of tuning. In week two the owner asks for Spanish: change order CO-01, 3 hours at $150 ($450) and 3 business days, signed the same afternoon before any work.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"A long, detailed proposal shows I am thorough.\" The proposal is a decision document; details belong in the SOW. Two to four pages with a clear choice beats twenty.","\"Absorbing small changes builds goodwill.\" Unpriced changes teach the client that scope is negotiable and drain margin; small changes still get a change order or draw down a prepaid bank.","\"If they seem interested, quote a price on the first call.\" Price unclear work only after discovery; for complex jobs sell a paid assessment first."],
problems:[
{q:"Your rates are 45% lead to discovery call, 55% call to proposal and 40% proposal to signature. How many leads a month do you need to win three new clients a month?", a:"Lead-to-client = 0.45 × 0.55 × 0.40 = 0.099. Leads = 3 ÷ 0.099 ≈ 30.3, so about 31 leads a month, or improve one stage (raising proposal-to-win to 50% cuts it to about 25)."},
{q:"Rewrite 'the website should be fast and work on phones' as testable acceptance criteria.", a:"For example: Largest Contentful Paint at most 2.5 s at the 75th percentile on mobile (PageSpeed Insights), no horizontal scrolling at 360 px width on the five key pages, tap targets at least 24 × 24 CSS px, and zero critical errors from an automated WCAG 2.2 AA check (axe) on those pages."},
{q:"A six-week website project is in week 11. The client keeps sending new pages, content arrived three weeks late, and nothing was ever written down. Diagnose it and fix it.", a:"The SOW had no client-responsibility dates, no out-of-scope list and no change process, so scope crept and delays were unattributed. Reset: freeze the original scope, issue a revised schedule showing the content delay, price the added pages as a change order, set an acceptance period, and use change control from here on."},
{q:"Mid-build, an AI receptionist client asks you to connect a second location's calendar. You estimate 4–6 hours. Write the change order's essential lines.", a:"Description (second location calendar integration, same booking rules), estimate (6 h at $150 = $900, or fixed $800 if you have done it before), schedule impact (+2 business days to go-live), assumptions (client provides API access within 2 days), and signatures and date; no work before signature."},
{q:"A client's wish list totals $12,500 against an $8,000 budget. How do you get to a signed SOW without discounting your rate?", a:"MoSCoW the list with the client: Musts go in phase 1 (up to $8,000), Shoulds become phase 2 or an option, Coulds go to a backlog, Won'ts are recorded. Keep the rate; reduce the scope, and show the phase-2 price so the client sees the path."}
],
checks:[{q:"Which document holds the legal terms that every SOW inherits?",a:"The master services agreement (MSA)."},{q:"What makes an acceptance criterion good?",a:"It is testable: a specific condition with an observable pass or fail."},{q:"What are the four BANT qualification checks?",a:"Budget, authority, need, timeline."}],
explain:"Role-play with ADA as a skeptical dental-office manager: run the first ten minutes of a discovery call, then explain to her why you will send a proposal with three options instead of quoting a price on the call."},

{id:"p54m3", title:"Pricing and Estimating: The Rate Formula, Utilization, Value Pricing and the Real Cost of Hosting and AI", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Derive a billable rate from target income, self-employment tax, benefits, overhead, profit and realistic utilization, and show its sensitivity.","Choose hourly, fixed, retainer, per-user, usage or value pricing for a job and say who carries the risk.","Estimate with three-point ranges and percentiles, and price a fixed fee from a P80 estimate.","Build operating-cost models for a hosting plan and an AI receptionist, including per-minute voice and token costs.","Compare hosted API costs with amortized local hardware, including peak concurrency, and decide which workloads belong where."],
body:`<p>What should you charge, and how do you know an offer makes money before you sell it? Most technical founders price by looking at competitors and shaving 10%. That is guessing, and it is how good engineers end up working 60 hours a week for less than they earned as employees. Pricing has a floor you can calculate (what it costs you to exist), a ceiling you can estimate (what the result is worth to the client), and a risk you can choose to carry or not. This module gives you all three, then applies them to the two offers whose costs surprise people most: hosting and AI.</p>
<h4>The rate formula, from what you need to live</h4>
<p>Start from the income you want, not from the market. A worked version for the model agency, a sole owner in Texas (no state income tax; the target pay is before federal income tax):</p>
<pre>owner pay target                       90,000
self-employment tax  90,000 × 0.9235 × 0.153   12,717
health insurance (placeholder)          6,000
retirement, 5% of pay                   4,500
overhead  2,400 a month × 12           28,800
total to cover                        142,017
revenue needed at 15% profit   142,017 ÷ 0.85 = 167,079
available hours   2,080 − 80 holidays − 120 vacation − 40 sick = 1,840
billable hours    1,840 × 0.60 utilization = 1,104
rate              167,079 ÷ 1,104 ≈ $151 an hour</pre>
<p>Self-employment tax is the employee and employer halves of Social Security (12.4%, up to the annual wage base) and Medicare (2.9%), applied to 92.35% of net self-employment earnings; half of it is deductible, and Module 4 covers the details. <strong>Overhead</strong> is every cost of running the business that belongs to no single client: software, insurance, accounting, marketing, hardware, phone, training. The model agency's $2,400 a month is $500 software, $250 insurance, $300 hardware amortization, $150 phone and internet, $200 accounting and legal, $700 marketing, $150 training and $150 miscellaneous. Divide the total to cover by billable hours and you get your <strong>loaded cost</strong>: 142,017 ÷ 1,104 ≈ $129 an hour. That is your break-even rate. Every hour you sell below it, you pay the client for the privilege of working.</p>
<h4>Utilization: the lever that moves everything</h4>
<p><strong>Utilization</strong> is billable hours ÷ available hours. It is the most sensitive input in the formula. At 45% the same business needs 167,079 ÷ 828 ≈ $202 an hour; at 75%, 167,079 ÷ 1,380 ≈ $121. A solo owner rarely sustains more than 60–65%, because selling, invoicing, learning, writing proposals and unbillable support take the rest of the week, and Maister's finder-minder-grinder roles (Module 1) all land on one person. Do not guess yours: track every hour for two weeks with a free tracker such as Clockify or Toggl Track, tagged billable or not (Lab 1). For an employee the same logic gives the loaded cost you charge against: a $25-an-hour technician costs about 1.3 times wage after payroll taxes, equipment and benefits, and at 75% utilization that is 25 × 1.3 ÷ 0.75 ≈ $43 per billable hour.</p>
<h4>Pricing models and who carries the risk</h4>
<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Risk sits with</th><th>Use when</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hourly (time and materials)</td><td>Client</td><td>Unclear scope, emergencies; cap it with a not-to-exceed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fixed fee</td><td>You</td><td>Defined scope you have delivered before</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retainer</td><td>Shared</td><td>Reserved capacity or advice every month; state rollover rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Per user or per device</td><td>You, scaled to the client</td><td>Managed IT and security</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usage (per minute, per call)</td><td>Client, above an included allowance</td><td>AI receptionists, metered APIs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Value pricing</td><td>You, rewarded for results</td><td>Outcomes you can quantify and influence</td></tr></table>
<p>Hourly billing punishes efficiency: the faster you get, the less you earn. Fixed fees reward it, which is why you move productized work to fixed prices as soon as you have measured it a few times. Raise prices on a schedule written into the MSA (for example annually, with 30 days' notice) so increases are routine, not confrontations.</p>
<h4>Value pricing</h4>
<p><strong>Value pricing</strong> sets the fee by the economic value of the result to the client rather than by your hours, an approach Alan Weiss's <em>Value-Based Fees</em> made famous among consultants. It depends on the discovery call (Module 2) having put a number on the problem. Bayou Line Plumbing loses about $23,000 a month of jobs to voicemail. If the receptionist recovers a third, that is about $7,700 of revenue; at a 40% gross margin for the plumber, about $3,000 a month of gross profit. A $549 subscription is under a fifth of the gain. Compare against the client's <em>profit</em>, not revenue, and show the arithmetic in the proposal: the price then defends itself.</p>
<h4>Estimating with ranges</h4>
<p>Give every work package a three-point estimate and use Domain 45's PERT: mean = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6, σ = (P − O) ÷ 6. For a small website: design (6, 10, 18 hours) gives 10.7 ± 2.0; build (14, 20, 36) 21.7 ± 3.7; content and SEO (4, 8, 16) 8.7 ± 2.0; launch and test (3, 5, 10) 5.5 ± 1.2. The means add to 46.5 hours. If tasks were independent the variances would add, σ = √22.8 ≈ 4.8, and the 80th percentile would be 46.5 + 0.84 × 4.8 ≈ 50.5 hours; if they all slip together (the same slow client), σs add instead, σ ≈ 8.8, and P80 ≈ 53.9 hours. Quote a fixed fee from the high end: 54 hours × $150 ≈ $8,100, so $8,000 with the scope pinned down. Early on the range is wider. McConnell's cone of uncertainty puts an initial-concept estimate anywhere from 0.25× to 4× the actual, an approved definition 0.5× to 2×, and completed requirements 0.67× to 1.5×, which is why Module 2 sells a paid assessment before a big fixed price.</p>
<h4>Operating-cost models: hosting and AI receptionists</h4>
<p>A care plan for a small website, per site per month: a $48 server shared by 20 sites ($2.40), offsite backup storage ($0.10), premium plugin licenses ($1.50), 20 minutes of updates and checks by a technician at $45 loaded ($15), and an incident reserve of one 3-hour cleanup a year ($11.25). Direct cost about $30 against a $79 price: a 62% gross margin, but only if someone other than you does the 20 minutes. At your own $129 the labor alone is $43.</p>
<p>An AI receptionist is metered. Each minute of a call uses telephony, speech-to-text, text-to-speech for the agent's half, often a voice-platform fee, and model tokens. Tokens surprise people the other way: each turn re-sends the system prompt, tool definitions and history, so a three-minute call can consume about 25,000 input tokens but only about 500 output tokens.</p>
<table><tr><th>Component (placeholder unit prices; use your vendors' current pages)</th><th>Per call-minute</th></tr>
<tr><td>Telephony, inbound</td><td>$0.010</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speech-to-text</td><td>$0.010</td></tr>
<tr><td>Text-to-speech, half the minute at $0.03</td><td>$0.015</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voice platform fee</td><td>$0.050</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model tokens: ~8,300 in and ~170 out per minute at $1 and $5 per million</td><td>$0.009</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>≈ $0.094</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>Voice, not the model, dominates. So price with included minutes and an overage rate, never "unlimited," and put the token arithmetic from Domain 34 in your spreadsheet so a model change does not silently erase your margin.</p>
<h4>API versus local hardware</h4>
<p>A Mac Studio with an M5 Ultra and 512 GB of unified memory (Domain 43: about 1.2 TB/s) can run very large open models. Cost it like a business asset. With a placeholder price of $10,000 (use your real quote) amortized over 36 months: $278 a month, plus about $11 of electricity (250 W for 10 hours and 30 W for 14 hours a day, about 89 kWh at $0.12), $25 for UPS, backup and connectivity share, and 2 hours of your administration at $129, $258. Total ≈ $572 a month. Tax depreciation may let you deduct the purchase sooner (Module 4), but the economic cost is spread over the machine's useful life.</p>
<p>The comparison is one formula: break-even tokens per month = local monthly cost ÷ API price per token. Against a hosted model at $3 per million input and $15 per million output, with 50 input tokens per output token, the blended price is about $3.24 per million, so break-even is about 572 ÷ 3.24 ≈ 177 million tokens a month. Twenty receptionist clients at 175 calls each use about 89 million, so on cost alone the API wins. Capacity is the second test. Local hardware is priced for peak load, APIs for use. A dense 70B model at 4-bit is about 40 GB of weights, so decoding tops out near 1,200 ÷ 40 = 30 tokens a second for the whole machine; four simultaneous callers share it, and Apple silicon is slowest at prompt processing, so a long context needs prompt caching (llama.cpp and MLX both support it) to keep time-to-first-token acceptable. If calls arrive at random (Poisson, Domain 14) averaging 0.875 at once during peak hours, four or more simultaneous calls happen about 1.2% of the time, which is too often to fail. The conclusion: run real-time voice on an API, or on a small, fast mixture-of-experts model locally with an API fallback. Put private, batch and steady workloads on the Studio: document assistants, transcript summaries, embeddings and internal agents (Domain 12). Local wins on privacy, control and predictable cost, not usually on price per token.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>ITIL AI Governance treats every AI deployment as a balance of value, cost and risk, and its capability model asks you to choose controls proportionate to the risk profile; this module supplies the cost side. The IMC USA Code of Ethics requires agreeing the basis of fees in advance and charging fees in line with the service delivered, and the CMC interview often probes how you priced an engagement.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A private document assistant for a 12-attorney firm.</strong> The firm wants questions answered from 400,000 pages of its own files. Hosted cost: embedding about 200 million tokens once is a few dollars at typical embedding prices; daily use of 40 questions × 22 days at 8,000 input and 600 output tokens is 7.0 million in and 0.53 million out, about $21 + $8 = $29 a month at $3 and $15 per million. Local cost: about $572 a month for the Studio, all-in. On tokens the API is twenty times cheaper. But the firm's confidentiality duties (the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct, Rule 1.05, and ABA Formal Opinion 512 of July 2024 on generative AI) make it want client files never to leave hardware it controls. So the decision is about risk and value, not tokens. You offer two tiers: Hosted ($6,000 setup + $650 a month, using a vendor with zero data retention and a signed data processing agreement) and Private ($14,000 setup + $1,400 a month: a dedicated Studio in the firm's office, encrypted at rest, on its network, monitored and backed up by you, with quarterly model updates). Your cost for the Private tier is about $572 a month, a 59% gross margin, and the firm chooses it because the price reflects what it is protecting.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"My rate is my old salary divided by 2,080.\" Divide by billable hours (often about 1,100) after adding self-employment tax, benefits, overhead and profit; the honest rate is usually two to three times the employee hourly figure.","\"A fixed price earns the same as hourly.\" A fixed price moves the estimating risk to you; set it from a high-percentile estimate, and only when the scope is defined.","\"Local hardware is free once it is bought.\" It costs amortization, power, administration and capacity limits; at small scale hosted APIs are often cheaper per token, and local wins on privacy, control and predictability."],
problems:[
{q:"Target pay $70,000, benefits $8,000, overhead $1,800 a month, 10% profit, 4 weeks off plus 10 holidays and 1 week sick, 55% utilization. Compute self-employment tax, revenue needed, billable hours and the rate.", a:"SE tax ≈ 70,000 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $9,891. Total = 70,000 + 9,891 + 8,000 + 21,600 = $109,491. Revenue = 109,491 ÷ 0.9 ≈ $121,657. Hours = (2,080 − 160 − 80 − 40) × 0.55 = 1,800 × 0.55 = 990. Rate ≈ $123 an hour."},
{q:"The model agency needs $167,079 a year and has 1,840 available hours. What utilization would make $125 an hour work, and is it realistic?", a:"167,079 ÷ 125 ≈ 1,337 billable hours, which is 72.6% utilization. Not realistic for a solo owner who also sells and administers; raise the rate, lower costs or add leverage."},
{q:"Four tasks have three-point estimates (2, 4, 8), (8, 12, 24), (3, 6, 12) and (1, 2, 5) hours. Compute the total PERT mean, σ assuming independence, the P80, and a fixed quote at $150 an hour.", a:"Means 4.33 + 13.33 + 6.5 + 2.33 = 26.5 h; σ² = 1 + 7.11 + 2.25 + 0.44 = 10.8, σ ≈ 3.3; P80 ≈ 26.5 + 0.84 × 3.3 ≈ 29.3 h; 29.3 × 150 ≈ $4,390, so quote about $4,500, more if the tasks are correlated."},
{q:"Telephony $0.0085, speech-to-text $0.006 and platform $0.05 per call-minute; text-to-speech $0.02 per agent-minute with the agent speaking half the time; 8,000 input and 170 output tokens per minute at $1 and $5 per million. Find the cost per minute and the margin on a $0.25 overage rate.", a:"0.0085 + 0.006 + 0.01 + 0.05 + 0.008 + 0.00085 ≈ $0.083 per minute. Margin = (0.25 − 0.083) ÷ 0.25 ≈ 67%."},
{q:"Your local inference box costs $600 a month all-in and the hosted alternative averages $2.50 per million tokens. Find the break-even, then decide for a workload of 40 million tokens a month.", a:"Break-even = 600 ÷ 2.50 = 240 million tokens a month. At 40 million the API costs about $100, so use the API unless privacy, contract or regulation requires local; revisit when volume grows about six times or when a client pays for the private tier."}
],
checks:[{q:"Define utilization.",a:"Billable hours ÷ available hours."},{q:"What is your loaded cost per billable hour?",a:"Total annual cost to cover (pay, taxes, benefits, overhead) ÷ billable hours; it is your break-even rate."},{q:"Break-even formula for local hardware versus an API?",a:"Local monthly cost ÷ API price per token = the monthly token volume at which they cost the same."}],
explain:"Show ADA your rate formula with your own numbers, then defend to her why you might run a law firm's document assistant on local hardware even though the API costs less per token."},

{id:"p54m4", title:"Legal, Risk and Money Basics in the US: Entities, Texas Filings, Contracts, Insurance and Taxes", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Compare a sole proprietorship and an LLC on liability, formation, taxes and paperwork, including the Texas Certificate of Formation, registered agent and assumed names.","Explain the Texas franchise tax and the sales-tax treatment of data processing, and list the questions to confirm with the Comptroller or a CPA.","Read an MSA for the clauses that allocate risk: limitation of liability, indemnity, IP ownership, warranties, confidentiality, payment and termination.","Match insurance (general liability, professional liability, cyber) to what you sell and to what contracts demand.","Plan self-employment tax and quarterly estimated tax, and name the moments to hire a lawyer or a CPA."],
body:`<p>What could take the business, or your house, away from you, and what paperwork prevents it? Technical founders tend to treat legal and tax work as a chore to postpone. That works until a client's server dies during your migration, a subcontractor claims your code, or the Comptroller asks why you never collected sales tax on hosting. <em>Read this first:</em> this module is general education as of October 2026, not legal or tax advice. Rules change, thresholds are adjusted, and your facts matter. Verify on the official sites in the listening library, and hire professionals at the points listed at the end. The goal is to make you a client who asks a lawyer or CPA the right questions, cheaply.</p>
<h4>Sole proprietorship or LLC</h4>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Sole proprietorship</th><th>Single-member LLC</th></tr>
<tr><td>Formation</td><td>Automatic when you start selling</td><td>File a Certificate of Formation with the state</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liability</td><td>Business debts and claims are your personal debts</td><td>Business debts and contract claims stay with the LLC if you keep it separate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Federal tax (default)</td><td>Schedule C; self-employment tax on profit</td><td>Same: a "disregarded entity" unless you elect S-corporation tax</td></tr>
<tr><td>Texas franchise tax</td><td>Not subject (a natural person)</td><td>Subject; most small LLCs owe nothing but must file a report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Name</td><td>Assumed name certificate with the county clerk if you use a name other than your own</td><td>The legal name is on the certificate; an assumed name is filed with the Secretary of State</td></tr></table>
<p>What the LLC does <em>not</em> do matters as much. It does not protect you from your own negligence, from debts you personally guarantee, or from a court disregarding it because you mixed personal and business money. Insurance and contracts carry most of the real risk; the LLC is the container that keeps one bad client from becoming a personal judgment. For a firm that touches other people's systems and data, most owners form one early.</p>
<h4>Texas specifics</h4>
<p>A Texas LLC is formed by filing a <strong>Certificate of Formation</strong> (Form 205 for a domestic LLC) with the Texas Secretary of State, online through SOSDirect; the filing fee is $300. The certificate names a <strong>registered agent</strong>, a person or company with a Texas street address who accepts legal papers on the LLC's behalf (you can be your own). Get a free employer identification number from the IRS, open a business bank account, and write a short company agreement even as a single member: it is your evidence that the LLC is a real, separate business.</p>
<p>The Texas <strong>franchise tax</strong> is a margin tax on entities with liability protection, LLCs included. The annual report is due May 15. Entities with total revenue at or below the no-tax-due threshold ($2.65 million for the 2026 and 2027 report years, per the Comptroller's franchise tax page; it is adjusted every two years) owe nothing and, since the 2024 reports, no longer file a No Tax Due Report, but they must still file a Public Information Report (or Ownership Information Report) every year. Above the threshold the rate is 0.75% of taxable margin (0.375% for retailers and wholesalers), or 0.331% of revenue under the EZ computation for revenue up to $20 million. Missing the information report can forfeit the LLC's right to do business, so put May 15 on the calendar now. A separate federal item: since a March 2025 FinCEN rule, US-formed companies are exempt from beneficial ownership (BOI) reporting; check whether that has changed when you form.</p>
<h4>Sales tax on digital services</h4>
<p>Texas taxes many services, and two categories matter here. <em>Data processing services</em> are taxable, with 20% of the charge exempt, so tax applies to 80% of the price; the Comptroller has generally treated website hosting and remotely accessed software as data processing. <em>Repair and maintenance of tangible personal property</em>, such as fixing a computer, is taxable in full. The state rate is 6.25% and local taxes add up to 2%, so in San Antonio (8.25%) a data processing charge carries an effective 0.80 × 8.25% = 6.6%. Consulting, design, training and managed-service bundles can be treated differently depending on what the client actually receives, and a bundle invoiced as one line may be taxed as its most taxable part. A sales and use tax permit is free and must be in place before your first taxable sale. Customers in other states follow their own states' rules: many tax no services at all, some tax SaaS, and since the Supreme Court's 2018 <em>Wayfair</em> decision a state can require you to register once your sales into it pass its economic-nexus threshold (often $100,000 a year). Before you set up invoicing, put each service line in writing to the Comptroller or a CPA: is it taxable, on what percentage, and how should the invoice itemize it?</p>
<h4>Contracts that allocate risk</h4>
<p>Your MSA (Module 2) is where risk is assigned. Read these clauses twice, in your paper and especially in a client's:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Limitation of liability</strong>: caps what either side can recover, typically at the fees paid in the 12 months before the claim, and excludes indirect and consequential damages such as lost profits. Common carve-outs (uncapped or separately capped) are breaches of confidentiality, indemnified claims and gross negligence or willful misconduct. Unlimited liability for a $1,500-a-month service is a bet-the-company term.</li>
<li><strong>Indemnity</strong>: a promise to defend and pay for the other side's losses from third-party claims, for example if your deliverable infringes someone's copyright. Keep it mutual and tied to your fault. Insurance policies commonly exclude liability you take on by contract beyond what you would owe anyway, so an over-broad indemnity can be uninsured.</li>
<li><em>IP ownership</em>: under the US Copyright Act, work by an independent contractor belongs to the contractor unless a signed writing assigns it; "work made for hire" covers employees and only nine narrow categories of commissioned work, which rarely include software. The standard agency position: the client owns client-specific deliverables on full payment; you keep your pre-existing tools and frameworks and license them; open-source components keep their own licenses.</li>
<li><em>Warranties</em>: a short workmanship warranty (say 30 days to fix defects) and a clear disclaimer that you do not guarantee uninterrupted service or the accuracy of AI-generated output.</li>
<li><em>Confidentiality and data protection</em>: plus a DPA or a HIPAA BAA where personal or health data flows (Module 6).</li>
<li><em>Payment and suspension</em>: deposits, net-15 terms, late fees and the right to suspend service for non-payment after notice.</li>
<li><em>Termination</em>: for convenience on 30 days' notice, with paid transition assistance (Module 5's offboarding).</li>
<li><em>Governing law and venue</em>: Texas law, Bexar County, and how disputes go (negotiation, mediation, then court or arbitration).</li></ul>
<p>Electronic signatures are valid under the federal ESIGN Act and the Texas Uniform Electronic Transactions Act, so a free e-signature tool is fine for routine SOWs and change orders.</p>
<h4>Insurance</h4>
<table><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>Pays for</th><th>Your example</th></tr>
<tr><td>General liability</td><td>Bodily injury, property damage</td><td>You knock a client's monitor off a desk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Professional liability (E&amp;O)</td><td>Financial loss caused by your work or advice</td><td>A migration loses a week of a client's email</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cyber, first-party</td><td>Your own breach response, ransomware, downtime</td><td>Your RMM account is compromised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cyber, third-party</td><td>Claims from others whose data was exposed through you</td><td>A client's patients are notified because of your error</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial auto</td><td>Accidents while driving for business</td><td>Driving to on-site support</td></tr></table>
<p>Technology firms usually buy <strong>errors and omissions</strong> (tech E&amp;O) and cyber together. Clients often require a certificate of insurance with limits such as $1 million per occurrence and $2 million aggregate. Tell the broker exactly what you sell: some policies exclude security testing or AI services unless they are declared. Texas lets most private employers opt out of workers' compensation, but a non-subscriber loses important legal defenses and must give notices, so price coverage before your first hire (Module 7).</p>
<h4>Money basics: taxes, cash and records</h4>
<p>Self-employment tax is 15.3% (12.4% Social Security up to the wage base, $184,500 in 2026, and 2.9% Medicare) on 92.35% of net earnings; half is deductible. Nobody withholds it, so you pay <strong>estimated tax</strong> quarterly with Form 1040-ES: April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15. You avoid the underpayment penalty if you owe less than $1,000 at filing or have paid at least 90% of this year's tax or 100% of last year's (110% if last year's AGI exceeded $150,000). If you also hold a W-2 job, extra withholding on your W-4 counts as paid evenly through the year, an easy way to cover side-business tax. The qualified business income deduction (up to 20%, made permanent in 2025) may apply, with limits for specified service businesses at higher incomes. An S-corporation election can reduce self-employment tax once profits are high enough to justify payroll and a reasonable salary; that is a CPA decision. Collect a W-9 from every subcontractor and file Form 1099-NEC when you pay one $2,000 or more in a year (the threshold rose from $600 for payments made in 2026 and later). Keep business money in its own account, reconcile books monthly in a tool such as Wave, QuickBooks or Xero, and build a cash reserve of three to six months of fixed costs.</p>
<h4>When to hire a lawyer or a CPA</h4>
<ul><li>A Texas business attorney: to turn your MSA and SOW templates into solid paper once (a fixed fee is common); whenever a client sends its own contract with unlimited liability, broad indemnity or IP assignment of your tools; before signing a BAA; before hiring or partnering.</li>
<li>A CPA or enrolled agent: at formation, to set up books and a sales-tax plan per service line; before the first estimated payment; before an S-corporation election; when out-of-state sales approach another state's threshold.</li>
<li>Free first stops: SCORE mentors and the Small Business Development Center network (UTSA hosts San Antonio's), which give no-cost advising.</li></ul>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>The CMC ethics exam and interview test conflicts of interest, confidentiality and fee agreements: exactly the clauses above. ITIL AI Governance's regulation area asks how law shapes governance policies and responsibilities, and Domain 26's ITIL supplier management is the same contract discipline seen from the buyer's side.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A first-year money map for a hypothetical single-member Texas LLC.</strong> Formation: Certificate of Formation filed through SOSDirect ($300), the owner as registered agent, a free EIN, a business checking account, a one-page company agreement and a free sales-tax permit. Revenue plan: $120,000; expenses $30,000; profit about $90,000, so self-employment tax ≈ 90,000 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $12,717. Last year's total federal tax was $9,800 on an AGI of $72,000, so the safe harbor is 100% of $9,800: $2,450 by each of April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15 (or the same amount as extra W-2 withholding), with the balance due next April. Sales tax: $3,000 a month of hosting and receptionist plans to Texas clients, treated as data processing, collects 3,000 × 0.80 × 8.25% ≈ $198 a month to remit. Franchise tax: revenue is far below the threshold, so no tax is due, but the Public Information Report is filed by May 15. Insurance: tech E&amp;O and cyber at $1 million each, plus general liability, because the first MSP client's contract demands a certificate. A subcontractor paid $3,000 for a migration gets a 1099-NEC, since that is over the $2,000 threshold. Two professional checkpoints are booked: a fixed-fee MSA review with a business attorney and a one-hour CPA session on sales tax by service line.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"An LLC protects my personal assets from everything.\" It shields you from the business's debts and many claims, not from your own negligence or personal guarantees, and a court can disregard it if you mix funds; insurance and contracts still do most of the work.","\"Digital services are not taxable in Texas.\" Texas taxes data processing services, which the Comptroller has applied to hosting and remotely accessed software, on 80% of the price; check every service line in writing.","\"The client paid, so the client owns the code.\" A contractor's work belongs to the contractor unless a signed writing assigns it; the contract must say who owns what, and when."],
problems:[
{q:"A San Antonio client's monthly invoice has $79 of hosting and a $449 AI receptionist plan, both treated as data processing at 8.25%. Compute the sales tax.", a:"Taxable base = 80% × $528 = $422.40; tax = 422.40 × 0.0825 ≈ $34.85 a month (an effective 6.6% of $528)."},
{q:"A client's MSA says: 'Contractor shall be liable for all damages arising from the services, without limit.' You are paid $1,500 a month. Propose a redline and explain it.", a:"Cap each party's liability at fees paid in the 12 months before the claim ($18,000); exclude indirect, consequential and lost-profit damages; mutual carve-outs for confidentiality breaches, indemnified IP claims and gross negligence or willful misconduct; align the cap with your E&O limit. If they refuse, have a lawyer review it or walk away."},
{q:"Last year's total tax was $9,800 on an AGI of $72,000. This year's profit will be much higher. What must you pay, and when, to avoid the underpayment penalty?", a:"100% of last year's tax: $2,450 by April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15 (or equivalent W-2 withholding). The rest is due with the return. If last year's AGI had exceeded $150,000, the safe harbor would be 110%, $10,780."},
{q:"You built a reusable AI receptionist framework. A client's contract says it will 'own all work product.' Draft your IP position.", a:"The client owns client-specific deliverables (its scripts, knowledge-base content, configuration and data) on full payment. You retain pre-existing and generally applicable tools, code and know-how, and grant the client a perpetual, non-exclusive license to use them as part of its deliverable. Third-party and open-source components keep their own licenses."},
{q:"You are about to take on a medical practice for managed IT with access to patient records. List the entity, contract and insurance steps before you start.", a:"Operate through an LLC with separate accounts; sign an MSA with a liability cap and a HIPAA business associate agreement (lawyer review); confirm tech E&O and cyber cover health data and are sized to the contract; get the certificate of insurance to the client; check the sales-tax treatment of the bundle; document your own HIPAA risk analysis (Module 6)."}
],
checks:[{q:"What Texas filing creates a domestic LLC, and what does it cost?",a:"A Certificate of Formation (Form 205) filed with the Secretary of State; $300."},{q:"When are federal estimated tax payments due?",a:"April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15 (the next business day if one falls on a weekend or holiday)."},{q:"Which insurance covers a client's financial loss from your professional mistake?",a:"Professional liability, also called errors and omissions (E&O)."}],
explain:"Walk ADA through the clauses you would never sign without reading twice (limitation of liability, indemnity, IP, payment, termination), then tell her the exact moments you would stop and hire a Texas business lawyer or a CPA."},

{id:"p54m5", title:"Delivering Managed IT and Hosting Reliably: SLAs, Monitoring, RMM, Backups, Patching, Incidents and Offboarding", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Write an SLA backed by measured SLIs and SLOs, compute error budgets, and set response targets a small team can meet.","Design monitoring and alerting that pages on symptoms, feeds a ticketing system and avoids alert fatigue.","Explain what RMM and PSA platforms do, choose tools, and secure them as the high-value targets they are.","Run backups with tested restores against stated RPO and RTO, and patch on a risk-driven cadence.","Communicate during incidents, keep runbooks and client documentation current, and offboard a client cleanly."],
body:`<p>What exactly have you promised, how will you know you are keeping the promise, and what happens at 2 a.m. when you are not? Recurring revenue (Module 1) is only worth something if delivery is boringly reliable, because churn is mostly a delivery problem. Domain 26 taught the service-desk vocabulary: tiers, tickets, priorities, SLAs and OLAs. Domain 21 taught incident response and Domain 30 taught backups. This module assembles them into the operating system of a small MSP and hosting business, sized for one or two people.</p>
<h4>Promise only what you can measure: SLI, SLO, SLA</h4>
<p>Google's SRE book separates three things that small providers blur. A <em>service level indicator</em> (SLI) is a measurement: the fraction of one-minute HTTP checks from an external monitor that return 200, with an expected keyword, in under 3 seconds. An <strong>SLO</strong> is your internal target for that indicator over a window: 99.9% per calendar month. The SLA is the contract: the promise to the client and the remedy if you miss it, normally service credits. Set the SLA looser than the SLO (say 99.5% against 99.9%) so you see trouble before it costs money, and make credits the sole remedy, capped, in line with Module 4's limitation of liability.</p>
<table><tr><th>Availability</th><th>Downtime allowed per month (30.44 days)</th></tr>
<tr><td>99%</td><td>about 7.3 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>99.5%</td><td>about 3.7 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>99.9%</td><td>about 43.8 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>99.95%</td><td>about 21.9 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>99.99%</td><td>about 4.4 minutes</td></tr></table>
<p>Dependencies multiply. A site on a host at 99.95%, behind DNS at 99.99%, running your application stack at 99.9%, is at best 0.9995 × 0.9999 × 0.999 ≈ 99.84%, about 70 minutes of expected downtime a month. Promising 99.9% on a single server is promising luck.</p>
<h4>Error budgets and what one person can promise</h4>
<p>The <strong>error budget</strong> is 1 − SLO: at 99.9%, about 43.8 minutes a month you are allowed to be down. It turns reliability into a spending decision. If 30 minutes are gone by day 10, you freeze risky changes (major plugin upgrades, server migrations) until the window resets and spend the time on the cause; if the budget is untouched, you can afford to ship. Response targets follow Domain 26's impact × urgency matrix:</p>
<table><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Example</th><th>First response</th><th>Updates</th></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Site or email down for everyone</td><td>30 minutes in support hours</td><td>Every 60 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>Major feature broken, key user blocked</td><td>2 business hours</td><td>Daily</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3</td><td>One user, workaround exists</td><td>8 business hours</td><td>On change</td></tr>
<tr><td>P4</td><td>Request or question</td><td>2 business days</td><td>On completion</td></tr></table>
<p>Be honest about coverage. A 24×7 P1 promise from a one-person firm means you never sleep and never take a vacation. Define support hours (for example 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. Central, Monday to Friday), sell after-hours coverage as a paid add-on, and arrange backup with a trusted peer MSP before you need it.</p>
<h4>Monitoring and alerting</h4>
<p>Watch what clients feel first: external synthetic checks of every site (status code and a keyword, from outside your network), TLS certificate expiry (alert at 21 days), domain expiry (alert at 60 days), DNS resolution, mail flow. Then causes: disk above 85%, memory, load, failed backup jobs and the age of the last good backup, endpoint health from the RMM (protection status, missing patches, failing disks), and identity alerts from Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace. Page only on symptoms a human must act on now; send everything else to tickets or dashboards. Every alert must be actionable; review noisy ones weekly and fix or delete them, because unactionable pages train you to ignore the phone. Free tools cover a small shop: Uptime Kuma for checks and a public <strong>status page</strong>, Prometheus with Alertmanager and Grafana (Domain 19), and ntfy for phone push. Run the monitor somewhere other than the thing it monitors, and give it a heartbeat to an outside service so you learn when the monitor itself dies.</p>
<h4>Tickets, RMM and PSA</h4>
<p>Give clients one way in: an email address and portal that create tickets, with an automatic acknowledgment stating the ticket number and the response target. Time logged on tickets feeds invoices and your utilization figure (Module 3). An <strong>RMM</strong> (remote monitoring and management) platform puts an agent on every managed endpoint for monitoring, patching, scripting, inventory and remote access. A <strong>PSA</strong> (professional services automation) platform runs tickets, time, contracts, billing and often CRM and projects. Commercial examples include NinjaOne, ConnectWise, Kaseya's Datto RMM and Autotask, N-able, Atera, Syncro, SuperOps and HaloPSA; for a lab or a very small start, Tactical RMM and MeshCentral are open source, and Zammad or FreeScout handle tickets.</p>
<p>Treat the RMM as the most dangerous system you own: it is a skeleton key to every client. In July 2021 the REvil ransomware group exploited Kaseya's on-premises VSA servers and pushed ransomware through dozens of MSPs to roughly 1,500 downstream businesses, and in May 2022 CISA and allied agencies issued joint guidance on protecting MSPs and their customers. Minimum controls: phishing-resistant MFA, separate admin accounts with least privilege, alerts on new administrators and on script deployments, logs kept off the platform, and the RMM itself patched first.</p>
<h4>Backups you have actually restored</h4>
<p>From Domain 30: keep three copies on two kinds of media with one offsite, and add the modern extension 3-2-1-1-0: one copy offline or immutable (object storage with object lock, or a backup server the clients cannot delete from), and zero errors after verification. State the RPO (maximum data loss) and RTO (maximum time to restore) per client in the SLA: a brochure site might be RPO 24 hours and RTO 4 hours; an office file server hourly snapshots and nightly offsite. Clients often assume Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace is backed up; retention policies and recycle bins are not backups, so sell and test a real one. Then do the arithmetic. Restoring 200 GB from offsite over a 100 Mbit/s link takes 200 × 8 ÷ 0.1 = 16,000 seconds, about 4.4 hours, before any rebuild: a 4-hour RTO needs a local copy. Test restores quarterly per client, record what was restored, how long it took and whether checksums matched. A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a control.</p>
<h4>Patching</h4>
<p>Run a monthly cycle aligned with Microsoft's Patch Tuesday (the second Tuesday): a pilot ring of your own machines and one tolerant client for two or three days, then everyone. Break the cycle for actively exploited vulnerabilities in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog, prioritized with Domain 21's KEV-and-EPSS logic. Include third-party applications and firewall and router firmware, and patch WordPress core and plugins weekly with a backup and a staging check first. Track patch compliance as a KPI (for example 95% of endpoints current within 14 days) and report it monthly; that report is what the client sees of your work. End-of-life systems are projects you sell: Windows 10 support ended on 14 October 2025, so any client still on it needs Extended Security Updates or an upgrade.</p>
<h4>Incident communication</h4>
<p>Clients forgive outages; they do not forgive silence. One person owns communication. The first notice goes out within 30 minutes for a P1 and says what is affected, what you know, what you are doing and when the next update comes. Updates follow on schedule even when nothing has changed. Do not speculate about cause or blame, and never promise a fix time you do not control. The resolution notice is followed within five business days by a blameless post-incident report (Domain 21): timeline, impact, root cause, fix, prevention and any credits due. If personal data may be involved, the contract and the law start clocks: under Texas law a company that holds data owned by someone else must notify the owner immediately after discovering a breach, and the owner must notify affected people within 60 days and the Texas Attorney General within 30 days if 250 or more Texans are affected.</p>
<h4>Documentation, runbooks and offboarding</h4>
<p>Keep one documentation space per client: contacts and escalation list, network diagram, asset inventory, credentials in per-client password-manager vaults, vendors and licenses (registrar, DNS host, ISP circuit IDs, the Microsoft 365 tenant), the backup map and standard procedures. A <strong>runbook</strong> is a step-by-step procedure for one recurring task or failure ("site down: check the monitor, DNS, host status, web server, application logs, then restore"), written so a subcontractor can follow it at 2 a.m. (Domain 26's writing rules). Offboarding is the mirror of onboarding and a test of character. Confirm the end date and final invoice; deliver documentation, configurations and a final backup export with checksums; hand over credentials, then rotate them; move the domain, DNS and hosting (domains should always be registered in the client's name, and registrar transfer locks can last 60 days, so start early); remove your delegated admin access in the Microsoft 365 tenant and your RMM agents; revoke your accounts and API keys; delete the client data you hold as the contract requires and confirm it in writing.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>ITIL Foundation (Domain 26) examines exactly these practices: service level management, monitoring and event management, incident management, the service desk, change enablement and supplier management. ITIL AI Governance's improvement model ends in implement and maintain: watching an AI service in production with the same SLO, alerting and incident discipline.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A Saturday outage, handled by the book.</strong> 09:12 Uptime Kuma pages: a client's site returns 503. 09:14 acknowledged; incident log opened. 09:20 status page set to "investigating" and a three-line email sent: site down since 09:12, cause under investigation, next update by 10:00. Diagnosis on the server: <code>df -h</code> shows the root file system 100% full and <code>du</code> finds a debug log that has grown to 18 GB because log rotation was never configured. 09:41 log truncated, rotation added, PHP-FPM restarted, checks green. 09:45 resolution notice. Monthly math: 33 minutes of downtime gives (43,830 − 33) ÷ 43,830 ≈ 99.925%, inside the 99.9% SLO with 10.8 minutes of error budget left, so plugin upgrades wait until next month. The SLA is 99.5%, so no credit is due. Tuesday's post-incident report records the root cause (no log rotation), the fix, and two preventions: a disk alert at 80% on every server, and log rotation added to the server build runbook.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Backups are running, so we are protected.\" A backup is proven only by a restore; test on a schedule, time it against the RTO and verify integrity.","\"A 99.99% SLA makes me look professional.\" It promises 4.4 minutes of downtime a month on infrastructure you probably cannot make that reliable; promise what your measured SLI supports, with an SLO tighter than the SLA.","\"More alerts mean better monitoring.\" Unactionable alerts train you to ignore the pager; page on user-visible symptoms and send the rest to tickets."],
problems:[
{q:"Your SLO is 99.95% per month and 15 minutes of downtime have already happened by day 12. How much budget remains, and what do you do?", a:"Budget = 0.05% × 43,830 ≈ 21.9 minutes; about 6.9 remain. Freeze risky changes for the rest of the month, spend time on the cause of the 15 minutes, and tell the client proactively if the SLA is at risk."},
{q:"A site depends on a host at 99.95%, DNS at 99.99%, a CDN at 99.9% and your stack at 99.9%. Estimate availability and recommend an SLA.", a:"0.9995 × 0.9999 × 0.999 × 0.999 ≈ 0.9974, about 99.74% or roughly 114 minutes a month in series (a CDN serving cached pages can raise it). Offer an SLA of 99.5% with an internal SLO near 99.7%, and remove single points of failure before promising more."},
{q:"A client's server holds 350 GB, backed up only offsite; the office has 200 Mbit/s down; the contract says RTO 4 hours. Is it achievable?", a:"Transfer alone: 350 × 8 ÷ 0.2 = 14,000 s ≈ 3.9 hours, with no time for rebuild or checks, so no. Add a local backup copy (a NAS or Proxmox Backup Server), restore critical shares first, or renegotiate the RTO, and prove it with a timed restore test."},
{q:"Design the alerting for a 15-person office you manage: what pages you at 2 a.m., what becomes a ticket, what goes to a weekly report?", a:"Page (if after-hours is purchased): internet or firewall down, server down, mail flow stopped, ransomware or EDR high-severity detection, backup repository unreachable. Ticket: failed backup job, disk above 85%, certificate expiring within 21 days, patch failures, a failing SMART disk. Weekly report: patch compliance, backup success and last restore test, licenses, risky sign-ins summary."},
{q:"A client leaves angrily for another MSP. List the offboarding steps in order and two things you must never do.", a:"Confirm end date and final invoice; deliver documentation, configs and a final backup export with checksums; hand over and then rotate credentials; transfer domain, DNS and hosting; remove tenant admin access and RMM agents; revoke your accounts and keys; delete retained data and confirm in writing; brief the new provider professionally. Never hold the domain or credentials hostage over an invoice dispute, and never keep copies of client data you were told to delete."}
],
checks:[{q:"SLI, SLO, SLA: one line each.",a:"SLI: the measurement; SLO: the internal target for it; SLA: the contract promise with remedies."},{q:"Downtime allowed per month at 99.9%?",a:"About 43.8 minutes."},{q:"What does an RMM platform do?",a:"Agent-based monitoring, patching, scripting, inventory and remote access across managed endpoints."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why your SLA promises less than your SLO, compute the error budget for one of your hosting plans, and tell her exactly what you do when half of it is gone by the middle of the month."},

{id:"p54m6", title:"Security and AI Services Done Responsibly: Authorization, HIPAA and PCI Clients, Recording Consent and Local versus Cloud AI", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Run an authorized security engagement as a business process: written authorization from the right person, scope, rules of engagement, data handling, reporting and retest.","Serve HIPAA and PCI DSS clients: know when you are a business associate, what a BAA requires, and how to keep card data out of scope.","Design an AI receptionist that respects call-recording consent law, robocall rules, biometric and privacy law and a written retention schedule.","Manage AI vendors with DPAs, BAAs, sub-processor lists and retention settings, and disclose AI use honestly.","Advise a client on local versus cloud AI across privacy, cost, quality, uptime and responsibility."],
body:`<p>Your two highest-margin service lines, cybersecurity and AI, are also the two that can end the business with one careless engagement. What separates a security consultant from a criminal is a signature. What separates a compliant AI receptionist from a wiretapping lawsuit is a sentence in the greeting and a clause in a vendor contract. This module is about the business process around those services: the documents, decisions and defaults. Techniques belong to Domains 8, 21 and 31, practiced only in sanctioned labs; here you learn to sell and deliver the work so that everyone, including you, is protected.</p>
<h4>Authorized security work is a paperwork discipline</h4>
<p>Domain 8's one rule, written permission first, becomes a business system. The federal Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and Texas Penal Code §33.02 (breach of computer security) make accessing a computer without the owner's effective consent a crime; your paperwork is what turns the same activity into a service. <strong>Written authorization</strong> must come from a person or entity with legal authority to approve testing of the specific systems; that may be an authorized representative rather than necessarily a corporate officer. The authorization and rules of engagement should name the testers, in-scope assets, dates, source addresses, permitted methods, exclusions, emergency contacts and stop conditions. A client cannot authorize testing of what it does not own: its web host's shared servers, its SaaS vendors' platforms, its ISP's equipment. The major cloud providers publish penetration-testing policies that allow testing of a customer's own resources with listed exclusions; read the current policy and keep a copy in the engagement file. Confirm in writing that your E&amp;O and cyber policies cover security services (Module 4); some exclude them unless declared.</p>
<h4>Scope, rules of engagement and the report</h4>
<p>The <strong>rules of engagement</strong> turn authorization into limits everyone can check:</p>
<ul><li><em>Scope</em>: exact domains, IP ranges, applications and test accounts in scope, and what is explicitly out.</li>
<li><em>Methods</em>: what is allowed, and what is prohibited unless separately authorized in writing (denial of service, social engineering, physical access).</li>
<li><em>Windows and sources</em>: testing hours and the addresses you test from, so the client's monitoring can tell you from an attacker.</li>
<li><em>Contacts and stop procedure</em>: named contacts on both sides, a way to halt immediately, and what happens if testing affects production.</li>
<li><em>Unexpected findings</em>: evidence of an existing compromise or illegal content means stop, notify the designated contact and preserve, not investigate further.</li>
<li><em>Data handling</em>: minimize what you collect, encrypt evidence, and set a destruction date.</li></ul>
<p>For small clients, start with what they can act on: a vulnerability assessment and configuration review against CIS Controls Implementation Group 1 and their Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace security settings (Domain 33), delivered as a prioritized risk report. Bring in a specialist partner for full penetration tests until you hold the skills and the credentials. Never sell "hacker-proof." Watch independence: if you also run the client's IT, you are testing your own work. Disclose that, and recommend an independent tester for the annual test; it is also a CMC ethics point. The report (Domain 8) leads with an executive summary in business language, then scope and method, findings with severity (CVSS, Domain 21), business impact and remediation, redacted evidence, and a retest section.</p>
<h4>Compliance-driven clients: HIPAA</h4>
<p>Medical and dental practices may be HIPAA covered entities when they meet the statutory definition. An MSP, hosting provider or AI vendor that creates, receives, maintains or transmits protected health information on behalf of a covered entity or business associate will generally be a business associate: an appropriate business associate agreement (BAA) and applicable HIPAA safeguards are required. The narrow conduit exception applies to transmission-only services with only transient access; a cloud provider that stores or processes ePHI generally remains a business associate even if the data is encrypted and it lacks the key. Downstream vendors may also be business associates when they handle PHI on your behalf. Confirm BAAs and product-plan terms in writing before any PHI flows, and obtain qualified compliance advice. Under the Breach Notification Rule, a business associate must notify the covered entity of a breach of unsecured PHI without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 calendar days after discovery; additional timing and content requirements may apply. HHS issued the HIPAA Security Rule Notice of Proposed Rulemaking on 27 December 2024 and published it on 6 January 2025. As of this fact-check (27 October 2026), HHS still lists it as a proposed rule and says the current Security Rule remains in effect; re-check HHS before advising a client because rulemaking can change. <a href="https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/faq/can-a-csp-be-considered-to-be-a-conduit-like-the-postal-service-and-therefore-not-a-business-associate-that-must-comply-with-the-hipaa-rules/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HHS guidance on cloud providers and the narrow conduit exception</a>.</p>
<h4>Compliance-driven clients: PCI DSS</h4>
<p>If a client takes cards on a website you built, design card data out of your systems. Use the payment processor's hosted payment page or embedded field so card numbers never touch the client's server; that keeps the merchant on the shortest self-assessment questionnaire and your hosting out of most of PCI DSS 4.0.1. You still protect the page that hands off to the processor. Never let an AI receptionist collect card numbers by voice unless the vendor provides PCI-validated capture that keeps digits out of recordings and transcripts; a single recorded card number pulls the whole call system into scope.</p>
<h4>AI receptionists: consent, disclosure and retention</h4>
<p>Recording law first. Federal law and Texas are <strong>one-party consent</strong>: one participant may record. About a dozen states require every party's consent, among them California, Florida, Illinois, Maryland, Massachusetts, Montana, New Hampshire, Pennsylvania and Washington, and with callers in many states the safe design assumes the strictest law applies. Golden Rock's clients take calls from everywhere, so every greeting discloses: "You've reached the automated assistant for Cedar Loop Dental; this call may be recorded." Continuing the call after a clear notice is how consent is usually obtained. California plaintiffs have also brought wiretapping claims against AI call-analytics vendors on the theory that the vendor is a third-party listener, which is one more reason to choose vendors that contractually do not use your clients' audio for their own purposes.</p>
<p>Disclosure of AI is the second layer. California's bot-disclosure law covers bots used to sell or persuade; Utah requires disclosure of generative AI in some interactions; the Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act, in force since January 2026, adds disclosure duties in settings including health-care services. The simplest compliant design is to say it is automated, always. Outbound calls are riskier: in February 2024 the FCC confirmed that AI-generated voices count as "artificial" voices under the Telephone Consumer Protection Act, so outbound AI calls need prior express consent (written consent for telemarketing), and business texting needs consent and carrier registration. Keep receptionists inbound unless consent is documented. Do not identify callers by voiceprint unless you must: Texas's biometric privacy law (CUBI) requires informed consent and limits retention, and the Texas Attorney General's $1.4 billion settlement with Meta in 2024 shows it is enforced.</p>
<p>Then retention: a written schedule per data type, enforced by automatic deletion. A reasonable default is recordings off or 30 days, transcripts 90 days, booking records in the client's own system under its own policy, and access logs 12 months; never collect Social Security or card numbers. Accuracy is a safety property too. Ground answers in an approved knowledge base (retrieval, Domain 34), refuse medical, legal or pricing promises the client has not approved, transfer emergencies to a human immediately, limit tools to what the job needs (book, reschedule, take a message), and run a scripted test set before launch and monthly, because a <strong>hallucination</strong> on a phone line is a promise made in the client's name.</p>
<h4>Vendors: DPAs, BAAs and where the data goes</h4>
<p>A <strong>data processing agreement</strong> (DPA) states what a vendor may do with personal data you send it: process only on your instructions, keep it confidential and secure, list and announce sub-processors, notify breaches, delete at the end, and allow audit. For every AI vendor, write down whether it trains on your data (business API terms usually say no; consumer apps often differ), how long it retains prompts and outputs, any zero-retention option, the processing region, and its sub-processors (the speech vendors behind the voice platform). Keep these in a vendor register (Domain 33's third-party risk) with a review date, and give clients a one-page summary: it wins deals with careful buyers.</p>
<h4>Local or cloud AI for a client</h4>
<table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Local (a Mac Studio or server you manage)</th><th>Cloud API</th></tr>
<tr><td>Privacy</td><td>Data stays on hardware you control</td><td>Depends on the DPA or BAA and the retention settings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Fixed: amortization, power, admin (Module 3)</td><td>Usage-based; cheap at low volume</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quality</td><td>Strong open models; behind the frontier on the hardest tasks</td><td>Frontier models available</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capacity and uptime</td><td>Sized for peak; one box is a single point of failure</td><td>Elastic and redundant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>You patch, secure, back up and, for PHI, carry Security Rule duties</td><td>Shared with the vendor under contract</td></tr>
<tr><td>Licensing</td><td>Check each open-weight license for commercial-use terms</td><td>Vendor terms of service</td></tr></table>
<p>The recommendation that usually survives scrutiny is hybrid: local for private, batch and steady work, cloud under a signed DPA or BAA for real-time and frontier tasks, with the routing rules written into the client's AI use policy.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>ITIL AI Governance's syllabus covers exactly this ground: AI risks and their controls, AI in organizational contexts, and how regulation shapes governance policies and responsibilities; its capability model sets controls by risk profile. The CMC ethics exam tests independence and confidentiality, and Domain 33's CIPT covers the privacy engineering underneath.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>An AI receptionist for a fictional San Antonio dental practice.</strong> Cedar Loop Dental wants after-hours and lunch-hour calls answered and appointments booked. Data flow: caller → telephony carrier → voice platform (speech-to-text, the model, text-to-speech) → the practice-management system's booking API → a summary to the practice's secure inbox. Callers give names, dates of birth and reasons for visits, so this is PHI. Agreements: a BAA between the practice and Golden Rock; subcontractor BAAs with the voice platform (available only on its business tier, which raises cost from about $0.07 to $0.11 a minute, passed through in the price), the model provider and the backup vendor; recordings disabled at the carrier so it never stores PHI. Greeting: "You've reached the automated assistant for Cedar Loop Dental. This call may be recorded. If this is an emergency, say 'emergency' to reach the on-call dentist." Rules: no clinical advice; swelling, bleeding or severe pain transfers to the on-call phone; no voiceprints; no card numbers. Retention: transcripts 30 days, summaries kept in the practice's own system, access logs 12 months. Before launch: 50 scripted test calls (bookings, reschedules, emergencies, attempts to make the assistant promise prices) with a pass threshold of 48 of 50, then 20 calls monthly. Golden Rock's own HIPAA risk analysis and the vendor register go in the client file, and the MSA's liability cap and the insurance certificate are checked against the BAA's terms by an attorney.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"The client said yes, so I am authorized to test its website.\" Only someone with authority over the specific systems can authorize, in writing; a client cannot authorize testing of its host's or SaaS vendor's infrastructure.","\"Texas is one-party consent, so the receptionist needs no disclosure.\" Callers in all-party-consent states such as California and Florida change the rule; disclose at the start of every call.","\"If the vendor encrypts the data, HIPAA does not apply to it.\" A vendor that stores or transmits PHI is a business associate even with encryption, and needs a BAA before any PHI flows."],
problems:[
{q:"A client asks you to 'test our online store this weekend.' The store runs on a hosted commerce platform; a small custom app runs on a VPS you manage. What can be authorized, what cannot, and which documents do you need?", a:"In scope, with written authorization from an officer: the custom app and VPS, and the client's own configuration in the platform admin. The platform's infrastructure is out unless its published policy allows testing (usually only through its own program). Documents: authorization letter, SOW, rules of engagement (scope, windows, source IPs, contacts, stop procedure, no denial of service), insurance confirmation and data-handling terms."},
{q:"A clinic's AI receptionist uses a telephony carrier, a voice-AI platform, a model API, your backup storage and a CRM. Which need BAAs, and what do you do about a vendor that refuses?", a:"Every party that creates, receives, maintains or transmits PHI: the voice platform, the model API (transcripts contain PHI), the backup storage, the CRM, and the carrier if it records or stores calls. You sign a BAA with the clinic and subcontractor BAAs downstream. A refusing vendor is designed out: switch vendors or stop the PHI reaching it (for example, disable carrier-side recording)."},
{q:"Write a retention schedule for an AI receptionist and justify each period.", a:"Recordings off, or 30 days for quality review; transcripts 30–90 days for tuning and dispute resolution; summaries and bookings in the client's system under its own policy; access and admin logs 12 months for security investigation; test-call records for the life of the contract. Deletion automated; no sensitive identifiers collected. Shorter retention means less to breach."},
{q:"A Texas client's receptionist recorded a California caller with no disclosure. Assess the risk and fix the system.", a:"California requires all parties' consent, so the caller may have a privacy claim, and vendors that use the audio can attract wiretap claims. Fix: add a recording and automation disclosure to the greeting, confirm the vendor's contract bars using the audio for its own purposes, review retention and delete what is not needed, and document the change and date."},
{q:"An 8-person accounting firm wants AI to summarize tax documents, mostly from February to April. Recommend local or cloud and justify it.", a:"Tax preparers fall under the FTC Safeguards Rule, so data protection must be documented either way. The load is seasonal and peaky, which favors a cloud API under a DPA with zero or short retention, sized elastically; a local box would sit idle eight months unless it also runs year-round private work. A hybrid (local for the most sensitive client files if volume justifies it) is reasonable; record the routing rules in the firm's AI policy."}
],
checks:[{q:"Who can authorize a security test?",a:"Someone with legal authority over the specific systems, in writing, naming testers, scope and dates."},{q:"What is a BAA?",a:"The HIPAA contract between a covered entity and a business associate (or a business associate and its subcontractor) that sets how PHI is used and protected."},{q:"Name three all-party-consent states for call recording.",a:"For example California, Florida and Washington (also Illinois, Maryland, Massachusetts, Montana, New Hampshire, Pennsylvania)."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA, as you would to a dental-office owner, why your AI receptionist announces that it is automated and may be recorded, which vendors had to sign BAAs, and how long each kind of data is kept."},

{id:"p54m7", title:"Growing the Business: Positioning, Reputation, Partners, Subcontracting, Hiring, Automation and the One-Page Operating Plan", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Write a positioning statement and choose two or three marketing channels that fit the niche and your capacity.","Build reputation honestly: reviews and testimonials within the FTC's rules, and case studies with measured results.","Use referral partners and white-label partners to extend what a small firm can credibly promise.","Decide between subcontracting and hiring with utilization, cash and classification tests, and compute a first employee's loaded cost.","Write a one-page operating plan with KPIs, review it quarterly, and make the choice to stay small deliberately."],
body:`<p>How does a one-person technical business grow without turning its owner into an exhausted manager of things he never wanted to manage? Growth is not one decision. It is a series of small ones about whom you serve, what they say about you, whom you partner with, when you add people, and what you refuse. Michael Gerber's <em>The E-Myth Revisited</em> names the trap: a skilled technician starts a business and then works <em>in</em> it forever instead of <em>on</em> it. This module turns the numbers from Modules 1 and 3 into a plan you can run, on one page, every quarter.</p>
<h4>Positioning before marketing</h4>
<p>Positioning is one sentence that tells the right client, at once, that you are for them: "For plumbing and HVAC companies with 3 to 20 technicians who lose jobs to voicemail, Golden Rock installs an AI receptionist that answers every call and books the job, live in ten business days, with a monthly report of recovered revenue." It names the client profile from Module 2, a problem with a number attached, the offer and the proof. Then choose two or three channels that fit your capacity and niche. For local managed IT: a complete Google Business Profile with reviews, local search, and referral partners. For a national productized offer: industry associations and trade events, LinkedIn, and one genuinely useful piece of content a month (a missed-call calculator beats ten generic blog posts). Marketing email must follow CAN-SPAM: identify yourself, include a physical address and honor unsubscribes within ten business days. Buy ads only after you know your funnel conversion rates, because only then can you predict customer acquisition cost (Module 1) before spending it.</p>
<h4>Reputation: reviews, testimonials and the rules</h4>
<p>Reviews are the cheapest marketing a small firm has, and the most regulated. Ask every satisfied client at the moment of visible success, such as the go-live report, and make it easy with a direct link. Respond to every review, including the bad ones, calmly and without disclosing anything about the client. The FTC's rule on consumer reviews and testimonials, in force since 21 October 2024, prohibits fake reviews and testimonials (including AI-generated ones), buying reviews conditioned on their sentiment, undisclosed reviews by owners and employees, and suppressing negative reviews, and it lets the FTC seek civil penalties. The FTC's Endorsement Guides add that a testimonial must reflect real experience and that any material connection, such as a discount for reviewing, must be disclosed. Google's own policy also forbids soliciting reviews selectively from happy customers only. The honest path is also the durable one.</p>
<h4>Case studies and the portfolio</h4>
<p>A <strong>case study</strong> is the proof behind the positioning: client and context, the problem with a baseline number, what you did, the measured result with dates, a quote, and what came next. "Missed calls answered within 60 seconds rose from 0% to 96% in the first month; 31 jobs booked, about $11,800 at the client's average ticket" persuades in a way "they were really happy" never will. Get written permission (the MSA should say you will not use a client's name or logo without consent), anonymize when you must ("a six-technician plumbing company in Florida"), and redact every screenshot. Build one portfolio page per industry you serve, following Domain 26's rules for evidence a stranger can check.</p>
<h4>Partnerships and referrals</h4>
<p>A <strong>referral partner</strong> serves your clients without competing with you: CPAs and bookkeepers, insurance agents (cyber-insurance applications ask security questions your clients cannot answer alone), web designers who do not want to host, office-equipment and telecom vendors, and other small MSPs for overflow and vacation cover. Agree how referrals are tracked and thanked; some professions restrict referral fees, so ask before offering one. A <strong>white label</strong> partner delivers a service under your brand: a managed detection and response provider whose 24×7 security operations center makes an MSSP offer honest (Module 1), an after-hours help desk, a specialist penetration-testing firm (Module 6). Distributors such as Pax8 and vendor programs such as the Microsoft AI Cloud Partner Program supply licenses, training and margin. Vet every partner as Domain 33 taught: security attestations (a SOC 2 report), insurance, BAA availability, SLA, and how you leave. You stay accountable to your client for everything a partner does in your name.</p>
<h4>Subcontracting and the first hire</h4>
<p>Subcontract first: it is a variable cost and buys specific skills. The agreement covers scope and rates, confidentiality, assignment of IP to you (so you can assign it to the client, Module 4), non-solicitation, insurance, and security rules: MFA, your password vault, no client data on personal devices. Classification is not a label you choose. The IRS looks at behavioral control, financial control and the relationship; the Department of Labor asks whether the worker is economically dependent on you. Someone who works only for you, on your schedule, with your tools, is probably an employee, and misclassification brings back taxes and penalties.</p>
<p>Hire when three signals agree: utilization above about 80% for three months while you turn work away, a pipeline that covers the salary at least one and a half times, and cash for three to six months of that salary. Price the hire at loaded cost. A $52,000 technician adds employer FICA of 7.65% ($3,978), federal unemployment tax ($42 on the first $7,000 after the usual credit), Texas unemployment tax at the 2.7% new-employer rate on the first $9,000 ($243), a health stipend ($4,800), tools and licenses ($2,400), training ($1,200) and a workers' compensation decision (about $500 as a placeholder): roughly $65,200, or 1.25 times salary. At 75% of 1,840 hours that is about $47 per billable hour, which is why managed-service labor in Module 1 was costed near $45.</p>
<h4>Tools and automation</h4>
<p>A small firm's back office is a stack: PSA and ticketing, RMM, documentation, a password manager, accounting, a CRM (HubSpot has a free tier), e-signature, a scheduling link, a time tracker, and an automation layer such as Zapier, Make or n8n, which you can self-host on your own hardware. Automate client onboarding (a form that creates the tickets and checklist), recurring invoices with card or ACH autopay (it lowers days sales outstanding), monthly client reports generated from the RMM, and your own phone line answered by your own AI receptionist, the best demo you will ever give. Automate a process only after doing it by hand three times and writing the runbook, measure the hours saved, and remember that every automation token is a credential to protect.</p>
<h4>Knowing when to stay small</h4>
<p>Paul Jarvis's <em>Company of One</em> argues that growth should be questioned, not assumed: grow when it makes the business better, not merely bigger. Measure profit per owner-hour, not revenue. A solo firm with subcontractors earning $120,000 on 40-hour weeks beats a five-person firm paying its owner $110,000 for 55-hour weeks, unless you want to build something sellable or lead a team. Staying small is a strategy with its own levers: raise prices, narrow the niche, productize, partner instead of hire. It also has risks to manage. Keep any single client under about a quarter of revenue, and plan for key-person risk, because the business stops when you do. It also leaves room for the rest of your plan in this app: the penetration-testing career path of Domains 8 and 31 can coexist with a small, profitable firm, and the security skills make the firm more valuable.</p>
<h4>The one-page operating plan</h4>
<p>The <strong>operating plan</strong> fits on one page and is reviewed every quarter: purpose in one line; ideal client and positioning; offers and prices (from Lab 1); 12-month targets; channels and the weekly sales activity that feeds them; capacity, partners and hiring triggers; the top three risks with mitigations; 90-day milestones; and the review rhythm (dashboard weekly, profit and loss monthly, plan quarterly).</p>
<table><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Formula</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>MRR and growth</td><td>Sum of monthly recurring fees</td><td>Predictability (Module 1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>MRR ÷ fixed monthly costs</td><td>Survival without new projects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Churn</td><td>MRR lost ÷ MRR at start of month</td><td>Delivery quality, compounded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gross margin by line</td><td>(Revenue − direct costs) ÷ revenue</td><td>Which offers to grow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utilization</td><td>Billable ÷ available hours</td><td>Pricing and hiring (Module 3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Days sales outstanding</td><td>Receivables ÷ revenue × days</td><td>Cash: $9,000 owed on $15,000 a month is 18 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cash runway</td><td>Cash ÷ fixed monthly costs</td><td>Keep three to six months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delivery health</td><td>Patch compliance, restore tests passed, SLA met</td><td>Leading indicators of churn (Module 5)</td></tr></table>
<p>The quarterly review is ITIL's continual improvement model (Domain 26) applied to yourself: where are we now, where do we want to be, how do we get there, did we get there?</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certification</h4>
<p>The CMC is the long game of this module: three years of engagements, five satisfied client evaluations and written engagement summaries are exactly the case studies and references you start collecting now, and its code of ethics requires honest representation of qualifications and results. ITIL AI Governance's final areas (applying AI governance across roles and functions, and aligning it with other frameworks) are what a growing AI practice puts in its own operating plan.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A quarterly review for the model agency.</strong> MRR is $8,500 against $6,400 of fixed monthly costs, a coverage of 1.33; churn averaged 1.2% a month; gross margin is 48% on managed IT, 71% on AI receptionists and 58% on care plans. Utilization was 78% over 13 weeks and $14,000 of project work was turned away. Cash is $21,000, a runway of 3.3 months. Option A, a full-time technician at about $65,200 loaded ($5,430 a month), would push runway under three months and needs about $5,400 a month of new gross profit to break even. Option B, a subcontractor for care-plan and patching work, about 40 hours a month at $45 ($1,800, variable), frees 40 of the owner's hours; at the $226 per hour contribution of AI receptionist work (Module 1), even half of those hours sold is about $4,500 a month. Decision: B now, and A when coverage passes 1.5 and utilization stays above 80% for a quarter with the subcontractor in place. Next-quarter milestones: three case studies with permission, two referral partners (a CPA and a cyber-insurance broker), a managed IT price increase at renewal, invoices moved to autopay to cut days sales outstanding from 24 to under 15, and a white-label after-hours partner before promising any 24×7 response.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Growth means hiring.\" Leverage can come from productizing, automation, partners and subcontractors; hire when utilization, pipeline and cash all agree.","\"A few glowing reviews from friends will get things started.\" Fake or undisclosed insider reviews violate the FTC's 2024 rule; ask real clients, disclose connections, and never buy or gate reviews.","\"A subcontractor carries the risk of their own work.\" You remain responsible to the client for everything done in your name, and a 'contractor' you control like an employee may be misclassified."],
problems:[
{q:"A first technician earns $48,000. Add employer FICA, $42 FUTA, Texas unemployment tax at 2.7% of $9,000, $4,200 benefits, $2,000 tools and $1,000 training. Compute the loaded annual cost, the cost per billable hour at 70% of 1,840 hours, and the billed rate for a 50% gross margin.", a:"FICA 48,000 × 0.0765 = $3,672; SUTA $243; total = 48,000 + 3,672 + 42 + 243 + 4,200 + 2,000 + 1,000 = $59,157. Billable hours = 1,288; loaded ≈ $45.93 an hour. A 50% gross margin needs about $91.86 an hour of billed value."},
{q:"Receivables are $12,600 and revenue over the last 30 days was $14,000. Compute DSO and give three ways to get it under 15 days.", a:"DSO = 12,600 ÷ 14,000 × 30 = 27 days. Fixes: autopay by card or ACH for recurring plans; deposits and milestone billing for projects; net-15 terms with invoices sent the day work is accepted, plus automatic reminders and a late fee in the MSA."},
{q:"Turn 'we helped a dental office with their IT and they were really happy' into a case-study outline with measurable results.", a:"Context: a fictional or permitted 4-dentist practice, 18 staff. Problem with baseline: unpatched PCs (62% compliance), no tested backups, 3 outages in a quarter. What you did: managed IT onboarding, patching rings, 3-2-1-1-0 backups with quarterly restore tests, BAA. Results with dates: patch compliance 97% in 60 days, zero unplanned outages in 6 months, 2-hour restore test passed. Quote, permission and next step."},
{q:"List the criteria and one practical test for choosing a white-label managed detection and response partner.", a:"Criteria: 24×7 SOC with stated response times, current SOC 2 Type II report, coverage of your clients' endpoints and Microsoft 365, clear escalation to you, BAA availability for health clients, insurance, pricing per endpoint, data location and retention, contract exit terms. Test: run a controlled detection exercise on your own lab endpoint with harmless test files and time how long the partner takes to alert you."},
{q:"Option A: five staff, $600,000 revenue, $110,000 owner profit, 55-hour weeks. Option B: solo with subcontractors, $260,000 revenue, $120,000 owner profit, 40-hour weeks. Compare profit per owner-hour over 48 working weeks and name two non-financial factors.", a:"A: 110,000 ÷ 2,640 ≈ $41.7 an hour. B: 120,000 ÷ 1,920 = $62.5 an hour. Non-financial: A builds a sellable firm and a team to lead, but adds management load and key-person dependence on staff; B keeps time free for other goals (such as the pentest path) but has a lower ceiling and more concentration risk."}
],
checks:[{q:"What does the FTC's 2024 rule on reviews prohibit?",a:"Fake or AI-generated reviews and testimonials, buying sentiment-conditioned reviews, undisclosed insider reviews and review suppression, among other practices."},{q:"DSO formula?",a:"Accounts receivable ÷ revenue for the period × days in the period."},{q:"Three signals that justify the first hire?",a:"Sustained utilization above about 80% with work turned away, pipeline covering the salary about 1.5 times, and three to six months of the salary in cash."}],
explain:"Present your one-page operating plan to ADA as if she were a SCORE mentor: your positioning sentence, the three KPIs you will watch weekly, and the evidence that would make you hire your first employee."}
],
listening:[],
labs:[
{id:"p54lab1", title:"Pricing model workbook: rate, utilization, overhead and offer margins", goal:"Build the spreadsheet that sets your prices from first principles and shows which offers make money.",
 steps:["In LibreOffice Calc (free from libreoffice.org) or Google Sheets, build an Inputs tab: target pay, health insurance, retirement percentage, twelve monthly overhead lines, profit percentage, holidays, vacation and sick days, and utilization. Compute self-employment tax as pay × 0.9235 × 0.153, the total to cover, revenue needed = total ÷ (1 − profit), available and billable hours, loaded cost per hour and the rate. Confirm the model inputs from Module 3 reproduce about $151 before entering your own.",
 "Add a Sensitivity tab: a two-way table of the rate against utilization (40–80% in 5-point steps) and target pay ($60,000–$120,000), with conditional formatting that turns any cell above your current rate red. Write down the utilization at which your current rate only breaks even.",
 "Add an Offers tab with one block per service line: hosting care plan (server share, backup, licenses, minutes of labor, incident reserve), AI receptionist (calls a month, minutes per call, per-minute telephony, speech-to-text, text-to-speech and platform prices copied from your vendors' current price pages, tokens per minute and price per million) and managed IT per user (tools and hours per user). Compute direct cost, gross margin and contribution per owner-hour, and flag any line under 50% gross margin.",
 "Add a Local-vs-API tab: your real hardware quote, months of useful life, watts under load and idle, the electricity rate from your bill and admin hours at your loaded cost. Compute the monthly cost and break-even tokens a month = local cost ÷ blended API price per token, and add a peak-concurrency check with POISSON.DIST for your expected average number of simultaneous calls.",
 "Track every working hour for ten working days in a free time tracker (Clockify or Toggl Track), tagging entries billable or non-billable by client. Export the CSV, import it into a Timesheet tab, compute measured utilization with SUMIFS, replace the assumed utilization with it, and note how the rate moved."],
 deliverable:"The workbook (.ods or .xlsx) with five tabs and a one-paragraph pricing decision: your rate, the offer you will grow, the offer you will reprice, and your local-versus-API rule."},
{id:"p54lab2", title:"Proposal, statement of work and deposit invoice for a sample client", goal:"Take a fictional client from discovery to a ready-to-sign SOW and a deposit invoice, using a docs editor and a free invoicing app.",
 steps:["Run a 30-minute mock discovery call with ADA playing the owner of Bayou Line Plumbing (Module 2) or a business you choose. Follow the Module 2 agenda, capture the numbers (calls missed, average job value, decision process, budget range) and send yourself the discovery summary email within the hour.",
 "Write a requirements table: at least ten functional and non-functional requirements and constraints, each tagged Must, Should, Could or Won't, plus Given/When/Then acceptance criteria for five deliverables.",
 "Estimate each work package with three points in your Lab 1 workbook, compute PERT means, σ and the P80 total, and price three options (good, better, best) including the monthly plan.",
 "In LibreOffice Writer or Google Docs, write a proposal of at most three pages and an SOW containing every section in Module 2's table, plus a one-page change-order form and the payment schedule. Export both as PDF.",
 "Create the client and a 50% deposit invoice in a free invoicing tool (Invoice Ninja, Wave or Zoho Invoice) with the payment terms and late-fee wording from your SOW; send it to your own email address and save the PDF."],
 deliverable:"Discovery summary, requirements table, estimate sheet, proposal PDF, SOW PDF with change-order form, and the deposit invoice PDF."},
{id:"p54lab3", title:"SLA, monitoring and incident-communication kit", goal:"Write the SLA you will actually sell, run free monitoring with a status page on your own machine, and rehearse an incident end to end.",
 steps:["Write a one-page SLA for a hosting and support plan: the SLI and how it is measured, monthly availability SLO and SLA, exclusions (announced maintenance), support hours, a P1–P4 table with response and update targets, service credits (capped, sole remedy) and client responsibilities.",
 "Add an appendix with monthly error budgets in minutes for 99.5%, 99.9% and 99.95%, and the series availability of your real stack (host, DNS, application).",
 "Install Docker, start Uptime Kuma (docker run -d --restart=always -p 3001:3001 -v uptime-kuma:/app/data --name uptime-kuma louislam/uptime-kuma:1) and a demo web server (docker run -d --name demo -p 8080:80 nginx). Add an HTTP keyword monitor for the demo and HTTP and certificate-expiry monitors for a site you own, create a status page, and connect one notification channel to your phone.",
 "Write four templates (initial notice, update, resolution, post-incident report). Then drill: stop the demo container, time detection, alert, acknowledgment and your first notice, start it again, and write the post-incident report with the measured timeline and the month's availability."],
 deliverable:"SLA PDF with the error-budget appendix, the four templates, screenshots of the monitors and status page, and the drill's post-incident report."},
{id:"p54lab4", title:"Client onboarding and offboarding runbook with a tested restore", goal:"Write the checklists that start and end every managed client, and prove your backup process with a timed, verified restore.",
 steps:["Write an onboarding checklist of at least 25 items in a spreadsheet (item, owner, due day, evidence): signed MSA and SOW, BAA or DPA if needed, emergency contacts, access through a password-manager vault (never by email), inventory of users, devices, licenses and vendors, domain and DNS confirmed in the client's name, monitoring, backups, patch ring, baseline security, documentation space and a welcome call.",
 "Create a sample client data set by copying 1–5 GB of your own non-sensitive files into a test folder. Install restic (restic.net; packaged for Homebrew, apt and Windows), initialize two repositories (a folder on an internal disk and one on a USB drive or second disk), back the folder up to both, and run restic check on each.",
 "Restore test: make a copy of the test folder, delete a subfolder in the copy, restore it from the USB repository with restic restore latest --target to a new location, time it, and verify integrity by comparing SHA-256 checksums of source and restored files. Record date, data, duration, checksum result and whether the RTO was met in a restore-test log.",
 "Write the offboarding checklist (final invoice, documentation and config export, final backup export with checksums, credential hand-over then rotation, domain, DNS and hosting transfer, tenant-admin and RMM removal, account and key revocation, data deletion with written confirmation) and run it on the sample client: export to a folder that stands for the new provider, delete your repositories with evidence, and write the deletion confirmation letter."],
 deliverable:"Onboarding and offboarding checklists, the restore-test log with timing and checksum evidence, and the data-deletion confirmation."},
{id:"p54lab5", title:"One-page operating plan, KPI dashboard and a mentor review", goal:"Write the plan you will run your business by next quarter, make its numbers live, and get an outside review.",
 steps:["Write the one-page operating plan from Module 7 in a docs editor: purpose, ideal client and positioning sentence, offers and prices from Lab 1, 12-month targets, channels and weekly sales activity, capacity and hiring triggers, the top three risks with mitigations, 90-day milestones and the review rhythm.",
 "Build a KPI tab in your Lab 1 workbook with formulas for MRR, coverage, churn, gross margin by line, utilization, days sales outstanding and cash runway, plus a 12-month MRR projection using your churn rate and planned new MRR a month; chart the projection.",
 "Write one case study with Module 7's structure, from a real past job with the client's written permission or anonymized, with a baseline number and a measured result.",
 "Book a free session with a SCORE mentor (score.org) or the UTSA Small Business Development Center, present the plan, record the three most useful pieces of feedback, and revise the plan."],
 deliverable:"The one-page plan as a PDF, the KPI tab and chart, the case study, and a short note of the mentor's feedback and what you changed."}
],
capstone:{title:"The operating system of your business: a complete service firm on paper",
 brief:"Assemble the operating package for your own business (use the model agency's numbers if you prefer to keep real figures private): a service catalog of three productized offers priced from your Lab 1 workbook, including the AI receptionist's per-minute cost model and your local-versus-API rule; an MSA outline and an SOW template with a change-order form, marked up with the questions for your attorney; an SLA with error budgets and incident templates; onboarding and offboarding runbooks with a dated, verified restore test; a compliance sheet for AI receptionists (recording and AI disclosure, retention schedule, vendor register with DPA and BAA status); and the one-page operating plan with live KPIs. Present it to ADA as you would to a lender or a prospective partner, and defend every number.",
 rubric:["Prices trace to the rate formula, measured utilization and per-offer cost models, and every offer shows its gross margin.","Scoping documents are complete: SOW sections, acceptance criteria, exclusions, client responsibilities, change control and payment terms.","The SLA is measurable (SLI, SLO, SLA, error budget) and its response promises are ones a small team can keep.","Risk is handled: entity and insurance choices, contract clauses flagged for professional review, recording consent, retention, DPAs and BAAs.","The operating plan has targets, KPIs with formulas, hiring triggers and a quarterly review date, and the restore test is evidenced."]},
exam:[
{id:"p54q1",type:"mc",q:"A client pays a flat $1,500 a month for you to keep its IT running, however many tickets it opens. Which model is this, and what incentive does it create?",options:["Break-fix; you earn more when systems fail","Managed services (MSP); you earn more by preventing problems","Freelance; the client manages the work","Value pricing; the fee depends on the client's profits"],answer:1,why:"A flat fee for ongoing responsibility is the MSP model, which rewards prevention: every outage avoided is margin kept."},
{id:"p54q2",type:"mc",q:"MRR is $10,000 and monthly churn is 2%. With no new sales, about how much MRR remains after 12 months?",options:["$9,800","$8,860","$7,600","$7,850"],answer:3,why:"10,000 × 0.98¹² ≈ $7,850; churn compounds, losing about 21.5% a year. $8,860 is the 1% case."},
{id:"p54q3",type:"mc",q:"A client generates $600 a month of gross profit, monthly churn is 1.5% and CAC was $3,000. What are LTV and payback?",options:["LTV $40,000; payback 5 months","LTV $9,000; payback 5 months","LTV $40,000; payback 20 months","LTV $4,000; payback 0.2 months"],answer:0,why:"LTV = 600 ÷ 0.015 = $40,000; payback = 3,000 ÷ 600 = 5 months."},
{id:"p54q4",type:"mc",q:"Which of these is a testable acceptance criterion?",options:["The receptionist should be friendly and helpful","The website must look modern","Given a free weekday slot, when a caller asks for an appointment, then it is booked in the shared calendar and an SMS confirmation is sent within one minute","The client will be satisfied with the automation"],answer:2,why:"Only the Given/When/Then statement has an observable pass or fail."},
{id:"p54q5",type:"mc",q:"During a fixed-price website project the client asks for four extra pages. What should you do first?",options:["Add them to keep the client happy","Refuse, because the scope is fixed","Send a change order with the price and schedule impact, and start only after it is signed","Add the hours to the final invoice"],answer:2,why:"Changes go through change control: priced, scheduled and signed before the work, so neither margin nor trust erodes."},
{id:"p54q6",type:"mc",q:"Where do the liability cap, IP terms and payment terms that apply to every project normally live?",options:["The proposal","Each change order","The discovery summary","The master services agreement"],answer:3,why:"The MSA holds the legal terms; each SOW inherits them and defines one project."},
{id:"p54q7",type:"mc",q:"You need $150,000 of revenue a year and have 1,800 available hours. At 50% utilization, what hourly rate is required?",options:["$83","$125","$167","$150"],answer:2,why:"Billable hours = 900; 150,000 ÷ 900 ≈ $167. $83 divides by all 1,800 hours, the classic mistake."},
{id:"p54q8",type:"mc",q:"A task's three-point estimate is O = 10, M = 16, P = 34 hours. What are the PERT mean and standard deviation?",options:["16 h and 4 h","18 h and 4 h","20 h and 8 h","18 h and 24 h"],answer:1,why:"Mean = (10 + 64 + 34) ÷ 6 = 18 h; σ = (34 − 10) ÷ 6 = 4 h."},
{id:"p54q9",type:"mc",q:"A local inference server costs $500 a month all-in and a hosted API averages $2 per million tokens. Below what monthly volume is the API cheaper?",options:["25 million tokens","1 billion tokens","250 million tokens","2.5 billion tokens"],answer:2,why:"Break-even = 500 ÷ 2 = 250 million tokens a month; below that the API costs less, before privacy and capacity are weighed."},
{id:"p54q10",type:"mc",q:"For a typical AI receptionist, which cost usually dominates per call-minute?",options:["Model tokens","Website hosting","Domain registration","Voice: telephony, speech-to-text, text-to-speech and platform fees"],answer:3,why:"In Module 3's model voice components are about 90% of roughly $0.094 a minute; tokens are about 10%."},
{id:"p54q11",type:"mc",q:"Which statement about a Texas LLC is accurate?",options:["It is formed by filing a Certificate of Formation with the Secretary of State and must name a registered agent","It is exempt from the Texas franchise tax","Below the threshold it must still file a No Tax Due Report every year","It protects the owner from liability for the owner's own negligence"],answer:0,why:"Form 205 creates it and names a registered agent. LLCs are subject to franchise tax; since 2024 no-tax-due entities file only the information report; and an LLC does not shield your own negligence."},
{id:"p54q12",type:"mc",q:"Texas taxes data processing on 80% of the charge. What is the tax on a $500 hosting invoice in San Antonio at 8.25%?",options:["$41.25","$33.00","$31.25","$25.00"],answer:1,why:"500 × 0.80 × 0.0825 = $33.00, an effective 6.6%."},
{id:"p54q13",type:"mc",q:"A contractor writes custom software for a client under a contract that says nothing about IP. Under the US Copyright Act, who owns the copyright by default?",options:["The contractor, unless a signed writing assigns it","The client, because it paid","Both jointly","Nobody until it is registered"],answer:0,why:"Commissioned software is rarely a work made for hire; without a written assignment the author keeps the copyright."},
{id:"p54q14",type:"mc",q:"An SLO of 99.9% over a 30.44-day month allows about how much downtime?",options:["4.4 minutes","21.9 minutes","43.8 minutes","7.3 hours"],answer:2,why:"43,830 minutes × 0.1% ≈ 43.8 minutes: the monthly error budget."},
{id:"p54q15",type:"mc",q:"Which practice best proves that backups work?",options:["Keeping three copies on two kinds of media","Encrypting the backup repository","Daily success emails from the backup software","A scheduled, timed restore with integrity checks against the RTO"],answer:3,why:"Only a restore proves recoverability; timing it tests the RTO and checksums test integrity."},
{id:"p54q16",type:"mc",q:"A client asks you to penetration-test its online store, which runs on a hosted commerce platform. What is correct?",options:["The client's written approval covers the platform too","The client can authorize only systems it owns or controls; the platform's infrastructure needs the platform's own permission or published policy","Testing is legal as long as nothing is damaged","Verbal permission from the store manager is enough"],answer:1,why:"Authorization must come from someone with authority over the specific systems, in writing; a customer cannot authorize testing of its vendor's infrastructure."},
{id:"p54q17",type:"sa",q:"Design the compliance controls for an AI receptionist that will answer calls for a medical practice from callers in several states.",answer:"Call content is PHI, so sign a BAA with the practice and subcontractor BAAs with every vendor that receives PHI (voice platform, model provider, carrier if it records, backup, CRM), and design out any vendor that refuses. Disclose at the start of every call that the assistant is automated and the call may be recorded, which covers all-party-consent states and AI-disclosure rules. Keep it inbound: outbound AI-voice calls need prior express consent under the TCPA. No voiceprints without CUBI-compliant consent and no card numbers. A written retention schedule with automatic deletion (for example transcripts 30 days, logs 12 months). Ground answers in an approved knowledge base, transfer emergencies to a human, limit tools and test with scripted calls before launch and monthly. Document your own HIPAA risk analysis and a vendor register with DPA and BAA status.",rubric:["Identifies PHI and the BAA chain including subcontractors, and what to do with a vendor that refuses","Recording and AI disclosure designed for all-party-consent states, and the inbound-versus-outbound TCPA rule","Retention schedule and data minimization (no card numbers, no voiceprints)","AI behavior safeguards: grounding, human handoff, limited tools, testing","Own obligations: risk analysis, vendor register, contract and insurance check"]},
{id:"p54q18",type:"sa",q:"As a sole owner you target $85,000 of pay, $10,000 of benefits, $2,000 a month of overhead and a 15% profit margin, with 1,840 available hours. Compute your rate at 60% utilization, explain what happens at 45%, and recommend how to price a care plan that needs 20 minutes of labor a month.",answer:"SE tax ≈ 85,000 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $12,010; total to cover = 85,000 + 12,010 + 10,000 + 24,000 = $131,010; revenue needed = 131,010 ÷ 0.85 ≈ $154,129. At 60%, billable hours = 1,104 and the rate ≈ $140; at 45% (828 hours) it is ≈ $186, so utilization is the lever. Loaded cost at 60% is about $119 an hour, so 20 minutes of your own labor costs about $40; with about $15 of other direct costs a $79 plan earns only about 30%. Delegate the work to a technician at about $45 loaded ($15 of labor, margin about 62%), automate the checks, or price the plan nearer $99.",rubric:["Self-employment tax and the total to cover computed correctly","Rate at 60% (about $140) and at 45% (about $186), with utilization named as the lever","Loaded cost used to cost the care plan's labor","A pricing or delegation decision that produces a healthy margin, justified"]}
],
cards:[
{id:"p54c1",front:"Productized service",back:"A fixed-scope, fixed-price, fixed-timeline package with a repeatable process, sold like a product (for example an AI receptionist live in ten business days)."},
{id:"p54c2",front:"Recurring revenue",back:"Revenue that repeats under a standing agreement without a new sale (hosting, managed IT, subscriptions); measured as MRR."},
{id:"p54c3",front:"Unit economics",back:"Whether one client is profitable on its own after every cost of serving it: ARPA, gross margin, CAC, payback and LTV."},
{id:"p54c4",front:"Gross margin",back:"(Revenue − direct costs) ÷ revenue, where direct costs are the per-client tools and loaded labor that scale with that client."},
{id:"p54c5",front:"Churn",back:"The share of recurring revenue or clients lost per month; a year's retention is (1 − churn)¹², so 2% a month loses about 21.5% a year."},
{id:"p54c6",front:"Discovery call",back:"A structured 30–45 minute conversation that quantifies the client's problem, desired outcome, constraints, budget and decision process before any price is given."},
{id:"p54c7",front:"Statement of work",back:"The project document under an MSA: objectives, scope and exclusions, deliverables with acceptance criteria, schedule, client responsibilities, fees and change process."},
{id:"p54c8",front:"Change order",back:"A signed amendment to the SOW stating a change, its price and its schedule impact; no work starts until it is signed."},
{id:"p54c9",front:"Acceptance criteria",back:"Testable conditions, often written Given/When/Then, that decide whether a deliverable is accepted."},
{id:"p54c10",front:"Utilization",back:"Billable hours ÷ available hours; the most sensitive input in the rate formula (solo owners rarely sustain more than 60–65%)."},
{id:"p54c11",front:"Overhead",back:"Business costs that belong to no single client: software, insurance, accounting, marketing, hardware, phone and training."},
{id:"p54c12",front:"Loaded cost",back:"The full cost of one billable hour: pay, taxes, benefits and overhead ÷ billable hours. For the owner it is the break-even rate."},
{id:"p54c13",front:"Value pricing",back:"Setting the fee by the economic value of the result to the client, measured against the client's profit, rather than by hours."},
{id:"p54c14",front:"Limitation of liability",back:"The MSA clause capping what a party can recover, typically fees paid in the prior 12 months, excluding indirect damages, with defined carve-outs."},
{id:"p54c15",front:"Indemnity",back:"A promise to defend and pay for the other party's losses from third-party claims, such as IP infringement; keep it mutual and tied to fault."},
{id:"p54c16",front:"Franchise tax",back:"Texas's margin tax on entities with liability protection, LLCs included: report due May 15; no tax below the threshold, but the Public Information Report is still filed."},
{id:"p54c17",front:"Estimated tax",back:"Quarterly federal prepayments of income and self-employment tax on Form 1040-ES, due April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15."},
{id:"p54c18",front:"Error budget",back:"1 − SLO: the downtime a service may spend in a window (about 43.8 minutes a month at 99.9%) before risky changes freeze."},
{id:"p54c19",front:"RMM",back:"Remote monitoring and management: agent-based monitoring, patching, scripting, inventory and remote access across client endpoints; secure it like a skeleton key."},
{id:"p54c20",front:"Runbook",back:"A step-by-step procedure for one recurring task or failure, written so someone else can follow it at 2 a.m."},
{id:"p54c21",front:"Written authorization",back:"Signed permission from someone with authority over the specific systems, naming testers, scope and dates; it is what makes security testing lawful."},
{id:"p54c22",front:"Business associate agreement",back:"The HIPAA contract required before a vendor creates, receives, maintains or transmits PHI for a covered entity; it flows down to subcontractors."},
{id:"p54c23",front:"Data processing agreement",back:"The contract setting what a vendor may do with personal data: instructions, security, sub-processors, breach notice, deletion and audit."},
{id:"p54c24",front:"Case study",back:"A proof story published with permission: client context, a baseline problem, what you did, measured results with dates, and a quote."},
{id:"p54c25",front:"Referral partner",back:"A non-competing business that serves your clients (a CPA, an insurance agent, a web designer) and refers work in both directions."},
{id:"p54c26",front:"Operating plan",back:"A one-page plan of positioning, offers, targets, channels, capacity, risks, milestones and KPIs, reviewed every quarter."}
]
});
